<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=84 g=1 f=1 -->
1.0&blank;Policy and Structure
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
1.1&blank;Introduction
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The Committee on National Accreditation of Environmental Laboratories (CNAEL) in its final report of September
1992 recommended the establishment of a national environmental laboratory accreditation program (NELAP). The
States function as the primary accrediting authorities and may contract with a third party as the accrediting body
for purposes of carrying out some parts of the accrediting functions, e.g. on-site inspections. As accrediting authorities,
the states would maintain the authority to grant accreditation, enforce compliance, etc. EPA shall oversee and approve
the state's compliance with all standards applicable to an accrediting authority. The recommended key elements
for NELAP include on-site assessments, performance evaluation testing, and data audits. To achieve the stated goals
of the CNAEL report, it is proposed to establish a National Environmental Laboratory Accreditation Conference (NELAC),
which is modeled after the National Conference on Weights and Measures. NELAC membership shall be voluntary and shall
be open to environmental laboratory accrediting authorities. The NELAC shall serve as the organization that shall
establish and modify the accreditation standards. Broad participation in NELAC shall identify laboratories which
are capable of providing reliable, uniform laboratory data which are acceptable to both Federal and State environmental
programs. National accreditation standards and procedures shall provide a level playing field where reciprocity
among the States in environmental laboratory accreditation shall be practicable. The creation of a National Environmental
Laboratory Accreditation Program allows coordination of the current accreditation activities of different States
or other governmental agencies, and reduces the number of on-site inspections, performance evaluation tests, and
related requirements with which the accredited organizations must comply. It is intended that NELAP function in
a manner which minimizes negative effects on the current accreditation operations of the States, requires minimum
outlay of State and Federal funds to implement, and that is self supporting.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
1.2&blank;Purpose of the Conference
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The National Environmental Laboratory Accreditation Conference shall be a standards setting body. NELAC shall,
through the process described, establish consensus uniform standards on which the national accreditation program
shall be based. These uniform standards shall include, but are not limited to, quality systems, performance evaluation,
audit programs, and other key elements as established by the standing committees of NELAC. It is NOT the purpose of
NELAC to function as an accrediting body, oversee or approve accrediting bodies, or administer any of the main elements
of the accreditation program.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
1.3&blank;Structure of the Conference
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The structure of the Conference is shown in Figure 1&hyph;1. The Board of Directors shall assume the overall supervisory,
administrative, and procedural duties. The Standing Committees and Administrative Committees are overseen by
the Board of Directors. The Standing Committees shall receive input regarding standards and test procedures, then
process this input into resolutions which shall be put before the Membership at the Annual Conference. These resolutions
shall be voted on by Active Members. The non-voting Contributors shall also have the opportunity to make presentations
and comments on the resolutions throughout the process and at the Annual Conference. The NELAC may also take into consideration
advice and comment provided to the Environmental Protection Agency through the Environmental Laboratory Advisory
Board (ELAB) chartered under the Federal Advisory Committee Act (FACA). The composition and relationships of these
bodies is described below.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1.3.1&blank;The Board of Directors
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00159 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The Board of Directors consists of the Conference Chair, the Chair-Elect, the most recent still active Past Chair,
the Treasurer, six members elected at large from the active membership (to serve 3-year staggered terms), an EPA official
to be appointed by the EPA Administrator as the NELAP Director (see section 1.4.1), and an Executive Secretary to be
named by the Director. The Board of Directors serves as a policy and coordinating body in matters of national and international
significance. The Board of Directors also makes interim policy decisions when necessary before the Voting Delegates
have an opportunity to vote on the issues in question.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1.3.2&blank;The Environmental Laboratory Advisory Board
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The ELAB consists of nine members composed of eight nongovernmental representatives and chaired by an EPA representative.
The members may be selected from a slate of nominees prepared by the Contributors' Committee. This FACA board advises
the EPA on matters affecting the interests of the contributors and other interested parties.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1.3.3&blank;The Committees
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The committees are the Standing Committees and the Administrative Committees. Both are overseen by the Board of Directors.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1.3.3.1&blank;The Standing Committees
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
These committees each consist of five members elected from the Active Membership of the Conference. They serve five
years and one new member is elected each year. The committee elects a chair. The committees shall generate standards
and policies for which they have responsibility to be presented at the annual Conference for vote. The committees
shall receive input via comments and presentations at the interim and annual conferences. The committees shall draft
resolutions which shall be published by EPA in the 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
Federal Register
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
. The committees shall prepare and arrange timely agendas for Interim Meetings and Annual Conferences.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
The Program Structure Committee.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 This committee shall develop modifications to the scope, structure, and requirements to the tiers and fields of testing.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
The Accrediting Authority Committee.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 This committee provides the standards used by EPA to approve state authorities.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
The Quality System Committee.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 This committee shall establish and keep current the key elements of a quality system including record keeping and
staffing requirements. The Committee shall also define uniform standard criteria for each of the elements of the
quality system.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00160 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
The Performance Evaluation Program Committee. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
This committee 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
shall determine the requirements for the Performance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Evaluation Program. The committee shall generate the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards for the Performance Evaluation Samples, provide 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
criteria for selection of the provider of the Performance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Evaluation Samples, and provide and update the protocol for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the use of Performance Evaluation Program in the accreditation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of laboratories. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
The On-Site Assessment Committee. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
This committee shall 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
establish the training and experience requirements of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessors; establish the frequency of inspections; and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
generate the procedures for on-site visits. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
The Accreditation Process Committee. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
This committee shall 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
establish and develop modifications for the accreditation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
process including the requirements for accreditation; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
procedural requirements for suspension, revocation and denial 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of accreditation; relative roles and responsibilities of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratories; and appeal processes. The Committee considers 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
matters concerning reciprocity of accreditation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
The Regulatory Committee. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
This committee provides the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Standing Committees with current information on Federal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulations which affect the testing that the laboratories do. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The Regulatory Committee annually presents a report for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Conference action. Its scope embraces all matters regarding 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the development and interpretation of uniform laws and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulations; the study and analysis of bills for legislative 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enactment; and the establishment and maintenance of published 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
guidelines and other effective means of encouraging uniformity 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of interpretation and application of laboratory accreditation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laws and regulations. This committee shall develop language 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which shall assist the states in the preparation and adoption 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of standardized statutes and regulations. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1.3.3.2&blank;The Administrative Committees 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The Administrative Committees, with the exception of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Contributors Committee, shall consist of members appointed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
from the active membership. The functions and the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
responsibilities of the Administrative Committees are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
described below. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
The Nominating Committee. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The Nominating Committee annually 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
presents a slate of nominees for all elective offices at the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
national annual conference. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
The Conference Management and Funding Committee. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
This 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
committee sets annual membership fees and conference 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
registration fees, manages the logistical details of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
interim meetings and annual conferences, prepares an annual 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
budget for the Conference to be submitted for approval to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Board of Directors, and publicizes the interim meetings and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
annual conferences. The Treasurer shall be an ex-officio 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
member of this committee. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
The Membership Committee. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
This committee initiates membership 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
invitations and publicizes the Conference to prospective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
members. The committee also provides coordination and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
participation of Contributors in all affairs of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Conference. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
The Fiscal Auditing Committee. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
This committee is responsible 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for the conduct and review of the annual audit of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Conference and shall report such findings to the Board of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Directors. It also audits the Treasurer's books annually. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
The Liaison Committee.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 This committee shall provide liaison 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
with other federal agencies such as the Department of Energy 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and the Department of Defense. In addition this committee 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
shall provide liaison with other national and international 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standard setting bodies such as the National Institutes of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Standards and Technology (NIST) and the International 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Organization for Standardization (ISO). The function of this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
committee is to provide and solicit information and develop a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
spirit of cooperation between NELAC and outside organizations. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00161 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
The Contributors Committee. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
This committee is composed of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
five Contributors. Its function is to serve as a focal point 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for the Contributors. The committee shall propose a slate of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
candidates to the EPA as potential appointees to the ELAB. It 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
solicits information from and provides feedback to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Contributors. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1.3.4&blank;The Membership
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The Membership consists of two classes_Active Members and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Contributors.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Active Membership. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Active membership is limited to State and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Federal Officials. The Active Members may vote and serve on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Committees. At the annual conference the voting Members 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are divided into a House of Representatives and a House of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Delegates. The House of Representatives is composed of one 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
officially designated State Representative from each State or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Territory, two representatives from each of seven EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Assistant/Associate Administrators (OSWER, OAR, ORD, OW, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
OPPTS, OECA, and OROSLR), and one officially designated 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Federal Representative from each other participating federal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program. The state representative should be the director of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the state environmental laboratory accreditation, or a high 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
level technically competent scientist who is knowledgeable 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
about environmental laboratory analysis and accreditation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
programs, or his or her designee. The Federal Representative 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is designated by the appropriate person in charge of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
federal program. All other State and Federal Officials are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
grouped as a body known as the House of Delegates. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Contributors. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The contributors are all other interested 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
parties and groups. They include, but are not limited to, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratory personnel, industry representatives, environmental 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
groups, the general public, laboratory associations, industry 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
associations, accreditation associations, and retired active 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
members. The Contributors may not vote, but can make 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
presentations, comments or input at all stages of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards and procedures making process. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1.3.5&blank;The Generation of Standards 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The standards for the accreditation of laboratories begin in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the various committees (see Figure 1&hyph;2). Draft standards 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed by the committees are published in the 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
Federal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Register 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
by EPA. After providing an appropriate time for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
review, an Interim Meeting is held and presentations, comments 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and other input are received. The draft proposals are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
processed and either presented at the Annual Conference or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
returned to committee for further work. These resolutions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
presented at the Annual Conference are voted upon by the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Active Membership. (See Constitution and Bylaws for voting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
procedures.) If rejected, they go back to committee for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reassessment or shelving. If approved, they are presented in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
Federal Register
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 in final form by EPA. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1.3.6&blank;Adoption of Standards 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Participating States must adopt the standards to maintain 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
status as a NELAP accreditor. If a State chooses not to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
participate in all or part of the accreditation program, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratories in that State may obtain certification from a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
participating State that is approved under NELAP. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
1.4&blank;Roles and Responsibilities of the Federal Government, the States, and Other Parties 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1.4.1&blank;Federal Government (USEPA) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The role of the federal government, as represented by the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
USEPA (the Agency), shall be that of oversight and evaluation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the accrediting authorities and that of administration of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
NELAP program elements which require a high degree of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standardization between different accrediting authorities. In 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
addition, the USEPA shall provide staff support to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Conference as provided for in the Bylaws and agreed to by the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Agency. The EPA shall assist the Conference by publishing in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
Federal Register
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 all proposed and final standards. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA will also evaluate state and federal laboratories to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assure compliance with NELAC standards. The EPA Administrator 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
will appoint a Director of the National Environmental 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Laboratory Program. The Director shall serve as an ex officio 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
member of the Board of Directors. He or she shall select a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
senior member of EPA with laboratory accreditation experience 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
as the Executive Secretary of the Conference (a full time 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
position). The Director's Office shall establish a program 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which evaluates, approves, and reports on the accreditation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
programs implemented by the state accrediting authorities. In 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
these reports, state accreditation programs shall be evaluated 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
against the national standard as established by the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Conference. The EPA shall evaluate, inspect, and approve state 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and federal laboratories as complying with NELAC standards. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In addition, the Agency shall establish a five member board, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Accrediting Authority Review Board, composed of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
representatives from the states, EPA, and other federal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
agencies, to review the process and procedures used by EPA to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
approve State and Federal laboratories and accrediting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
authorities. It is recommended that the Agency provide 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
administrative support to a performance evaluation sample 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program so as to ensure uniformity of sample composition and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
performance evaluation standards. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1.4.2&blank;State Governments 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00162 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
State governments shall be the primary accrediting authority. The state's Laboratory Accreditation Program will
be audited and approved by the Director's Office. As the accrediting authority, states will have full responsibility
for ensuring conformance with the national standard established by NELAC. States will be responsible for accrediting
applicant organizations through approving applications, performing on-site assessments and maintaining performance
evaluation sample programs. States are responsible for ensuring that on-site inspectors are trained in accordance
with NELAP requirements. States shall submit the names, and appropriate accreditation material, to the EPA for inclusion
in the National Laboratory Database. States may choose to contract accreditation activities to a third party (non-government)
agency. If contracted to a third party, states remain the accrediting authority and retain responsibility for ensuring
compliance with the standards established by NELAC. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1.4.3&blank;Joint Federal and State Roles 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The NELAC (Conference) shall be the joint responsibility of the Federal Government (Agency) and the state accrediting
authorities. As provided in the following section on structure of the Conference and the Conference Bylaws, state
accrediting authorities and the Agency share responsibilities of governance, analysis and establishment of policy,
and analysis and establishment of technical standards as they apply to the NELAP. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1.4.4&blank;Other Parties 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
All other interested parties including, but not limited to, the laboratory industry, clients of the laboratory industry,
environmental or other public interest groups, and the general public, shall function as contributors to the Conference.
In this role, these other parties shall bring technical and policy issues to the attention of the Conference, its managing
Board, or its subcommittees. It is anticipated that these issues shall be brought to the Conference in the form of reports,
presentations, discussion material, or other forms of documentation for presentation at the annual Conference,
committee, or subcommittee meetings. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
1.5&blank;Scope of the Program 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The scope of the National Environmental Laboratory Accreditation Program shall encompass the necessary scientific
testing to serve all U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) monitoring, compliance or other functions mandated
by statutes and pursuant regulations. Some of the statutes are the Federal Insecticide, Fungicide and Rodenticide
Act (FIFRA); the Safe Drinking Water Act (SDWA); the Resource Conservation and Recovery Act (RCRA); the Comprehensive
Environmental Response Compensation and Liability Act (CERCLA): the Federal Water Pollution Control Act (Clean
Water Act); the Clean Air Act (CAA); and the Toxic Substances Control Act (TSCA). In addition, the program shall also
include provisions to permit special requirements or fields of testing promulgated by any of the States and/or Territories.

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
However, the program shall not be implemented or administered in a way which limits the ability of local, state or federal
agencies to investigate and prosecute enforcement cases. Specifically, when engaged in the collection and analysis
of forensic evidence to support litigation those agencies may use any procedure that is appropriate given the nature
of the investigation, subject only to the bounds of sound scientific practice. This program shall not apply to those
government laboratories engaged solely in the analysis of forensic evidence. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00163 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
1.6&blank;Structure of the Accreditation Requirements 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The structure of the NELAP shall be based on the field of testing (see Figure 1&hyph;3). It shall consist of a set of general
requirements that all applicants must satisfy. Applicants for a particular field of testing must also meet the necessary
number of additional levels of specific requirements or functions that are linked to the general requirements. The
number and the degree of difficulty of the required additional levels shall depend on the complexity of the test procedures
in question. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
It is proposed that the different fields of laboratory testing be structured into groupings based on parameter, group
of parameters, or method. In addition, a category of supplemental accreditation will be designated. A ``supplemental''
accreditation means accreditation of a laboratory which has met additional methods or parameters required by a state
accrediting authority.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
``Supplemental'' accreditation shall be needed only for those few methods and/or parameters which are unique to
a particular state. These supplemental requirements shall be limited in number and scope. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1.6.1&blank;General Requirements 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The general requirements are applicable to all applicants regardless of their size, volume of business, or field
of testing. The organizational structure, or procedures used by applicant organizations to meet these general requirements
may differ as a function of size or scope of testing of an organization. The general requirements shall include all
the elements outlined in 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
General Requirements for the Competence of Calibration and Testing Laboratories,
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 ISO/IEC Guide 25: 1990 (E). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
General requirements shall include Health and Safety, and Waste Management Programs. Applicant organizations
shall be required to be in compliance with all applicable federal, state, and local rules and regulations covering
environmental, and occupational health and safety. Responsibility for the evaluation of compliance with these
rules and regulations shall remain with the appropriate regulatory body. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The relevant elements listed in the document are as follows: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1.6.1.1&blank;Organization and Management 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The organization shall be legally identifiable; the organization shall have managerial staff with the authority
and resources needed to discharge their duties; this includes technical management with overall responsibility
for the technical operations, and quality management with responsibility for the quality system and its implementation.

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1.6.1.2&blank;Quality System, Audit and Review 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The organization shall establish and maintain a quality system appropriate to the type, range and volume of calibration
and testing activities it undertakes; the quality manual, and related quality documentation, shall state the organization's
policies and operational procedures; the organization shall arrange for audits of its activities at appropriate
intervals to verify that its operations continue to comply with the requirements of the quality system. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1.6.1.3&blank;Personnel 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The organization shall have sufficient personnel, having the necessary education, training, technical knowledge
and experience for their assigned functions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1.6.1.4&blank;Accommodation and Environment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Organization facilities shall have suitable space, energy sources, lighting, heating and ventilation for proper
performance of calibrations or tests. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1.6.1.5&blank;Equipment and Reference Materials 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The organization shall be furnished with all items of equipment (including reference materials) required for the
correct performance of calibrations and tests. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1.6.1.6&blank;Measurement Traceability and Calibration 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00164 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Standards used for calibration must be traceable. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1.6.1.7&blank;Calibration and Test Methods 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The organization must document instructions on the use and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operation of all relevant equipment. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1.6.1.8&blank;Handling of Calibration and Test Items 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The organization must document a system used to identify the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
items to be calibrated or tested. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1.6.1.9&blank;Records
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The organization shall maintain a record system to suit its 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
particular circumstances and comply with any applicable 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulations. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1.6.1.10&blank;Certificates and Reports 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The organization certifies and reports the calibration and/or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
test results. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1.6.1.11&blank;Sub-Contracting of Calibration or Testing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The organization shall sub-contract work only to organizations 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that are accredited by a NELAC accrediting authority. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Subcontractors must be clearly identified. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1.6.1.12&blank;Outside Support Services and Supplies 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The organization must use only those outside support services 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and supplies that are of adequate quality. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1.6.1.13&blank;Complaints 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The organization shall have documented policy and procedures 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for the resolution of complaints received from clients or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
other parties about the organization's activities with records 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
maintained of all complaints and of the actions taken by the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
organization; where a complaint, or any other circumstance, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
raised doubt concerning the procedures, or other requirements 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or otherwise concerning the quality of the organization's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
calibrations or tests, the organization involved is promptly 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
audited in accordance with pre-established procedures. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1.6.2&blank;Specific Requirements Linkage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Additional tiers of requirements can be linked to the general 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements. To illustrate the tiered approach, a schematic 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
representing the accreditation scope and structure by field of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
testing is given in Figure 1&hyph;3. It indicates that all NELAP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
applicants must meet the basic requirements. Additional 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
specific tiers of requirements are linked to the basic 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements for a particular test or activity. An 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
organization seeking accreditation in hazardous waste organic 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
testing must meet all the requirements listed in basic 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements, general laboratory, organic, and hazardous 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
waste. The specific and detailed requirements under this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
scheme have not been developed at this time. The appropriate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and necessary requirements of the various tiers and fields of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
testing will be developed by the Program Structure Committee. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1.6.3&blank;Discussion 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The field of testing structure proposed for the national 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
environmental laboratory accreditation program provides 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
flexibility. This allows for the incorporation of new methods 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or new instrumentation without the applicants repeatedly 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
demonstrating the basic requirements that the accreditation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
applicants have previously satisfied. Redundancy of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
qualification assessment is avoided. Avoidance of redundant 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reviews and assessments shall significantly expedite the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
processing of applications which cover different fields of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
testing. Such a scheme provides a structure whereby 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
appropriate and specific accreditation requirements can be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
established to meet the prevailing needs of environmental laws 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and regulations. Regulators are thus provided with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
environmental sample testing results generated by laboratories 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
according to specified or equivalent methods and quality 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assurance protocols. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Additionally, the adoption of parameter, method specific and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
supplemental classifications allows for the design of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accreditation to suit needs of individual laboratories and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
states. This flexibility shall promote reciprocity among all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the participating States. The field of testing approach 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed shall also allow for the future incorporation of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
performance based methods (PBM) by substituting an approved 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
PBM for the specified analytical methods. Any supplemental 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements essential to meet state needs would be added at 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the parameter or method specific level. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
1.7&blank;Funding of the Program 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Funding shall be needed to cover the costs arising from at 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
least three areas: the administration and functions of NELAC; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
expenses incurred by EPA through its oversight and related 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
administrative duties; and expenses incurred by the States 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
because of accreditation functions including on-site visits, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
performance evaluation samples, processing applications, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
other duties. Funding mechanisms for each of these cost areas 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is proposed below: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00165 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1.7.1&blank;Self Supported NELAC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The NELAC should be self-sustaining financially insofar as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
possible. The Interim meetings and Annual Conferences 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
expenses should be financed by registration fees and annual 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
dues for Members and Contributors. These dues and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
registration fees should be set by the Conference Management 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and Funding Committee. Other expenses of committee members 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
shall be paid by their organizations. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1.7.2&blank;EPA Program Support
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The EPA should provide support for the National Environmental 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Laboratory Accreditation Program. This program includes 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
oversight and evaluation of accreditation authorities, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
evaluation and approval of state and federal laboratories, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
administrative support for the Conference, and publications in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
Federal Register
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1.7.3&blank;Fee Supported State Programs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
All costs of state accreditation programs may be covered 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
through the collection of application fees from the applicant 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
organizations. Such fees would cover the cost of application 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and processing, performance evaluation, site assessments, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
staff training, Conference membership and participation, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
other appropriate activities, whether such activities were 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
carried out directly by the state accrediting authority or by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
contract to a third party. It is recommended that a dual fee 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
structure be implemented by the state authorities. A full fee 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
should be charged applicants for which the state is the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
primary accreditor. A reduced fee should be charged 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
applicants for which the state is the secondary accreditor. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
This fee structure is based on the principle that fees shall 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cover the actual cost of an accreditation. The primary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accrediting authority shall incur the full cost of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accreditation. The secondary accrediting authority, having 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accepted the accreditation of another authority through 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reciprocity, shall only incur the cost of registration of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accredited organization. Costs incurred by a secondary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accrediting authority related to supplemental requirements, as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
described in section 1.8.2, should be reflected in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
supplemental fees. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
1.8&blank;Reciprocity 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
All member accrediting authorities shall grant reciprocity to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
all other member accrediting authorities which have met the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
national standard. This principle of reciprocity is an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
element of the national accreditation standard, to which all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
member accrediting authorities are held. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Reciprocity among the environmental laboratory accrediting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
authorities is essential to the success of a national program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The principal accrediting authorities shall be the states. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The states or federal agencies which act as accrediting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
authorities, must accept accreditation from other accrediting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
authorities in order for a national uniform program to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
succeed. Three policy issues are presented which are key to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
acceptance of the reciprocity principle by accrediting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
authorities. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1.8.1&blank;Fair Representation of Accrediting Authorities 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The accrediting authorities must have a fair and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
representative voice in the National Environmental Laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Accreditation Conference. NELAC shall establish the basic 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
scope, structure, and standards of the national program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Acceptance of the national program, in lieu of state programs, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
shall be significantly enhanced by fair and meaningful 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
participation of state accrediting authorities in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
establishment of the national program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1.8.2 Scope and Essential Quality Standards 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The national program (the national consensus standard) adopted 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
by NELAC shall have a scope and essential quality standards 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which meet or exceed the requirements of the existing state 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accrediting authorities. NELAC must consider the range of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
scope and quality systems requirements of the state 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accrediting authorities in the adoption of a national program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
A national program which falls significantly short of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
existing state program requirements, shall either not be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accepted by state authorities, or shall require such extensive 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
state supplementary requirements as to make the national 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program irrelevant. It is recognized that certain state 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
authorities shall have special requirements which arise from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a unique statutory, economic, or ecological situation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Reciprocity shall be possible if state mandated supplementary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements are limited in number and complementary to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
national program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00166 </DOCNO>
