<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00167 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=84 g=1 f=1 -->
2.0&blank;Performance Evaluation Testing Program 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
2.1&blank;Enrollment in PE Testing Program 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Each laboratory must enroll in a performance evaluation (PE) testing program that meets the criteria detailed by
the National Environmental Laboratory Accreditation Program (NELAP). The laboratory must participate in an approved
program or programs for each field of testing for which it seeks accreditation. Participation shall mean the analysis
and reporting of all test samples. Laboratories shall participate in PE testing for all fields of testing at a frequency
determined by the NELAC standards. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The laboratory must notify the accreditation agency of the NELAP-approved program or programs in which it chooses
to participate to meet PE testing requirements. For those tests performed by the laboratory for which PE testing is
not currently available, the laboratory must establish and maintain the accuracy and reliability of its testing
procedures by a system of internal quality management. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For each field of testing for which the laboratory seeks accreditation, it must participate in the designated, NELAP-
approved PE testing program for at least twelve months before designating a different program. The laboratory must
notify the primary accreditor before any change in designation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Laboratories shall bear the cost of any subscription to a PE testing program required by NELAP. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Each participant must authorize the PE testing program to release to the primary accreditor all data required to determine
the laboratory's compliance with the criteria. The primary accreditor shall make individual performance results
available to all requesters. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
2.2&blank;Approval of PE Testing Programs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
In order for a PE testing program to receive approval, the program must be offered by a Federal or State agency, or entity
acting as a designated agent for the Federal or State agency. A Federal or State program seeking approval or renewal
for its PE program for the next calendar year must submit an application to the NELAP director providing the required
information by July 1 of the current year. The program must provide technical assistance to resolve problems that
the participants experience such as anomalies during analysis of the samples, lost samples, or receipt of broken
sample containers, etc. In addition, the PE testing program must, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) Assure the quality of test samples, appropriately evaluate and score the PE test results, and identify performance
problems in a timely manner; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) Demonstrate to the primary accreditor (or NELAP) that it has: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(1) The technical ability required to: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
i. Either prepare samples or evaluate samples purchased from manufacturers, who prepare the samples in conformance
with the appropriate good manufacturing practices; and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ii. Distribute samples with at least two levels of analytes. Rigorous quality control must assure that samples mimic
actual environmental samples when possible and that samples are homogeneous and remain stable over the period of
testing. Stability shall be verified by routine testing on stored samples, within the time frame for analysis by PE
test participants. Samples shall be maintained by the PE testing program to retest laboratories with unsatisfactory
performance, or which have significant changes in accreditation status; 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00168 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(2) A scientifically defensible process for determining the correct result for each challenge offered by the program;

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(3) A program of sufficient challenge, with a frequency of no less than two times per year, to establish that a laboratory
has met performance requirements; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(4) The resources needed to distribute, analyze and interpret individual laboratory performance. The PE program
will provide:
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
i. Individual results to the laboratories, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ii. Statewide and nationwide reports to regulatory agencies on individual laboratory performance on PE test events,

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
iii. Cumulative reports and scores for each laboratory, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
iv. Reports of specific laboratory failures using grading criteria acceptable to NELAP,which must be provided on
a timely basis. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(5) Provisions on each PE report form used by the laboratory to record PE results, an attestation statement that PE
test samples were tested in the same manner as routine samples, with a signature block to be completed by the individual
performing the test as well as by the laboratory management; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(6) A mechanism for notifying participants of the PE shipping schedule and for participants to notify the PE testing
program within three days of the expected date of receipt of the shipment that samples have not arrived or are unacceptable
for testing. The program must have provisions for replacement of samples that are lost in transit or are received in
a condition that is unacceptable for testing; and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(7) A process to resolve technical, administrative, and scientific problems about program operations; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) Provide and maintain the following documentation as described: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(1) Reports of PE test results and all scores for each laboratory's performance (an electronic or a hard copy, or both)
must be provided to the primary accreditor, NELAP, and the participating laboratory in the format required by NELAP
within 60 days after the date by which the laboratory must report PE test results to the PE testing program; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00169 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(2) Records of each laboratory's performance must be maintained for a period of five years or such time as may be necessary
for any legal proceedings; and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(3) An annual report must be provided to the primary accreditor and NELAP with, if needed, an interim report, which
identifies any previously unrecognized sources of variability in kits, instruments, methods, or PE samples, which
may adversely affect the ability of the primary accreditor or NELAP to evaluate laboratory performance. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
If a PE testing program is determined by NELAP to fail to meet any criteria for acceptance as an approved performance
evaluation testing program, NELAP will notify the PE testing program and the primary accreditor. The PE program must
notify all laboratories enrolled in their PE program of the nonapproval and the reasons for nonapproval, within 30
days of the notification. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
2.3&blank;Testing of Samples 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The laboratory must examine or test, as applicable, the PE samples it receives from the PE testing program in the same
manner as it tests environmental samples, and return the results by the deadline stated in the sample package. The
analyst testing or examining the samples and the laboratory management must attest to the routine integration of
the samples into the workload using the laboratory's routine methods. The laboratory must test samples the same number
of times that it routinely tests environmental samples. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Laboratories that perform tests on PE samples must comply with the following restrictions and limitations on communications
and sample transfer: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) Laboratories must not engage in any interlaboratory communications pertaining to the results of PE sample(s)
until after the date by which the laboratory must report the results to the PE program for the PE test event in which the
samples were sent; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) Laboratories with multiple testing sites or separate locations must not participate in any communications or
discussions across sites/locations concerning PE sample results until after the date by which the laboratory must
report the PE test results to the program; and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) The laboratory must not send PE samples or portions of samples to another laboratory for any analysis for which
they seek accreditation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Any laboratory that the primary accreditor or NELAP determines intentionally referred its PE samples to another
laboratory for analysis and submits the other laboratory's results as their own, will have its certification revoked
for a minimum period of one year. Any laboratory that receives PE samples from another laboratory for testing must
notify the accreditation program of the receipt of those samples. Laboratories not doing so may have their accreditation
suspended for a period not to exceed one year. This policy is not intended to prevent interlaboratory testing designed
as part of a methods development or evaluation study, and applies only to PE samples. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The laboratory shall initiate chain of custody procedures upon receipt of all PE samples. The laboratory must maintain
a copy of all records, including analytical worksheets, for a minimum of five years. This record must include a copy
of the PE program report forms used by the laboratory to record PE results, and an attestation statement signed by the
analyst and the laboratory management stating that PE samples were tested in the same manner as routine samples. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
2.4&blank;Scoring 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Option I: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Pre-established pass/fail range set by calculating 95% confidence interval determined by previous studies. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00170 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Option II: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Statistical evaluation of data from all participants in the current study. Calculation of 95% and 99% confidence
intervals to set marginal and unsatisfactory performance. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Option III: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Pre-established pass/fail intervals as established in 40 CFR 136, appendix B. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Option IV: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The following scoring protocol applies to: All chemical analytes; bacteriology samples that require quantitation
(total and fecal coliform in non-potable water); fibers in air determined by phase contrast microscopy; asbestos
in friable solid material by polarized light microscopy; and asbestos in air and potable water by transmission electron
microscopy. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The true values may be established through robust statistical analysis of the results reported by all laboratories,
in order to reject gross outliers and establish a mean result and standard deviation, or through results obtained
by a panel of 12 reference laboratories (this is done for asbestos in friable material). A laboratory's result on a
given sample is then assessed as: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Good 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
if it is within the 95% confidence interval about the mean, or reported as ``less than'' the method detection limit
if the sample is a blank; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Marginal 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
if it is outside the 95% confidence interval, but within the 99% confidence interval about the mean, or reported as
``less than'' twice the method detection limit; or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Unsatisfactory 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
if it is any other result. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For each test, a laboratory receives 2 PE samples for each certified analyte. On two consecutive tests, a laboratory
must obtain a passing score of at least 75% on the 4 samples analyzed, calculated by applying the following formula.

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->

<!-- PJG STAG 4725 -->

<IMPORT>

<!-- PJG importformat s,w233,d28 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=95 g=1 f=1 -->
E:GRAPHICSEN02DE94.012
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</IMPORT>

<!-- PJG /STAG -->

<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Hence, the laboratory must obtain at least two good results plus two marginal results, or three good results plus one
unsatisfactory result, over two consecutive tests. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00171 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
In response to the accreditation program guidelines, certain chemistry analytes are scored by taking fixed intervals
about the known target value, where good performance is defined as a result within those fixed target intervals, and
unsatisfactory performance is any other result. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For the potable water total coliforms, where qualitative analysis is required (i.e., presence/absence), a laboratory
is required to maintain an average passing score of 90% on two consecutive tests. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Laboratories being tested for the determination of radon in air are required to submit 5 sampling devices to the PE
testing program. Four of these are exposed to a known concentration in a standard atmosphere exposure chamber, and
the remaining device is left unexposed as a ``blank''. The devices are then returned to the laboratories for analysis,
and they are required to report results within 25% of the target value on at least 4 of the 5 devices. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
2.5&blank;Successful Participation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Each laboratory must successfully participate in a PE testing program approved by NELAP for each field of testing
in which the laboratory is accredited. If a laboratory's accreditation is suspended or revoked because it fails to
participate in PE testing for one or more fields of testing, or voluntarily withdraws its accreditation for the failed
field of testing, the laboratory must then demonstrate satisfactory performance on two consecutive PE test events,
one of which may be onsite, before the primary accreditor will consider it for reinstatement. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Laboratories shall agree to test additional samples at the option of the primary accreditor for the following situations:

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) A major change in ownership or supervision of the laboratory; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) Complaints by users or employees; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) Unsatisfactory performance on most recent PE test event; or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(d) Request by the laboratory to be reinstated in a field of testing. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Failure to participate in a PE test event shall result in an automatic rating of unsuccessful performance and results
in a score of zero for the PE test event. Consideration may be given to those laboratories failing to participate in
a PE test event only if: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) Routine testing was suspended during the time frame allotted for testing and reporting PE test results; and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) The laboratory notifies the primary accreditor and the PE testing program within the time frame for submitting
PE test results of the suspension of routine testing and the circumstances associated with failure to perform tests
on PE samples. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Failure to return PE test results to the PE program within the time frame specified by the program is unsuccessful performance
and results in a score of zero for the PE test event. The PE testing program will specify the conditions and procedures
for late submissions, e.g., lost or broken samples. For those late submission categories, the participant will be
allowed to test the samples on an alternate schedule. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For any unsatisfactory PE test event for reasons other than a failure to participate, the laboratory must undertake
appropriate training and employ the technical assistance necessary to correct problems associated with a PE test
failure. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00172 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Remedial action must be taken and documented, and the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
documentation must be maintained by the laboratory for five 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
years from the date of participation in the PE test event. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Failure to achieve an overall PE test event passing score for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
two consecutive PE test events or two out of three consecutive 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
PE test events is unsuccessful performance. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=84 g=1 f=1 -->
3.0 On-Site Assessment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
3.1&blank;Introduction 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The on-site assessment is an integral part of a lab 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accreditation program and will be one of the primary means of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
determining a laboratory's capabilities and qualifications. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
During the on-site assessment, the assessment team will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
collect information and make observations which will be used 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to evaluate the laboratory's conformance with established 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accreditation criteria. It is essential that the on-site 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessment be conducted in a uniform, consistent manner 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
throughout the nation to facilitate reciprocity among States, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and for the laboratory community to accept the accreditation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
process. This section contains proposals and recommendations 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for conducting on-site assessments. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
3.2&blank;On-Site Assessment Personnel 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.2.1&blank;Training 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The National Environmental Laboratory Accreditation Conference 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(NELAC) will specify the minimum level of education and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
training for assessors, including refresher/update training. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The NELAC will also develop criteria for training 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements. The assessor training course will be developed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and implemented by EPA, NIST, or a non-Federal entity with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
oversight by EPA. A state may develop and implement it's own 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessor training program, subject to EPA oversight, if the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
state program can meet the NELAC standards. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.2.2 Qualifications 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
A laboratory assessor may work for a Federal, State, or a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
third party accrediting body. An assessor, including each 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
member of an inspection team, must be an experienced 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
professional and hold at least a B.S. degree, or equivalent 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
education and experience, in the specific discipline being 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
evaluated. Each assessor must also have satisfactorily 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
completed a laboratory accreditation training course and a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
health and safety training course, and take periodic 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
update/refresher training, as specified by NELAC. Each new 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
candidate assessor must undergo on-the-job training during one 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or more inspections until judged proficient. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.2.3&blank;Additional Qualifications 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
In addition, the assessors must: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) Be familiar with the relevant legal regulations, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accreditation procedures, and accreditation requirements; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00173 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(b) Have a thorough knowledge of the relevant assessment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
methods and assessment documents; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) Be technically conversant with the specific tests or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
types of tests for which the accreditation is sought and, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
where relevant, with the associated sampling procedures; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(d) Be able to communicate effectively, both orally and in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
writing; and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(e) Be free of any commercial interest that might cause the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessor to act in other than an impartial or nondiscriminatory manner. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.2.4&blank;Assessor Certification 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Before an assessor can conduct on-site inspections, the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
individual must be certified to do so, in writing, by either 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the NELAP or State in which the individual will assess 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratories. For each laboratory inspection performed by a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
state-designated third party assessor (i.e. non-EPA, non-State), the assessor must sign a statement before the

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inspection, certifying that no conflict of interest exists. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
3.3&blank;Frequency of On-Site Assessments 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.3.1&blank;Frequency 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Accreditors should perform a routine on-site assessment at 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
least annually. Assessments may be more frequent at 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratories where a problem exists, including complaints 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
about laboratory quality, questions of fraud, or recurring 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
failure on performance evaluation samples. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.3.2&blank;Follow-Up Evaluations 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
In addition to routine evaluations, assessors may need to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
conduct one-time follow-up evaluations at laboratories where 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a significant deficiency was identified by the previous 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
evaluation. These evaluations may be limited to determining 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
whether a laboratory has corrected its deficiency(ies), or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
determining the merit of a formal appeal from the laboratory. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
When deficiencies may result in downgrading of accreditation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
status, follow-up evaluations should occur as soon as possible 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
but no later than 60 days after the original evaluation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.3.3&blank;Changes in Laboratory Capabilities 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The accrediting authority may also deem necessary a limited 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
one-time evaluation when a major change occurs at a laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in personnel, equipment, or a laboratory location that might 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
impair analytical/biological capability and quality. A major 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
change in personnel is defined as the loss or replacement of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the laboratory management staff, or loss of a trained and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
experienced individual who performs a particular test for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which accreditation has been granted. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.3.4&blank;Announced and Unannounced Visits 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The accrediting authority is not required to provide advance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
notice of an assessment. However, the policy is to provide 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
such notification, based on the circumstances of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
particular assessment and laboratory. Since these highly 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
technical assessments may involve sensitive information and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
because there is a need to ensure that appropriate personnel 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and records are available for assessment, the testing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratory usually is notified in advance of a planned 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessment. The accrediting authority, at its discretion, may 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
conduct unannounced evaluations for cause (e.g., questions of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fraud, tips, complaints, or problems with performance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
evaluation samples) or as part of a routine practice. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
3.4&blank;Pre-Assessment Procedures 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.4.1&blank;Introduction 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
A good assessment begins with planning, which should commence 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
well before the assessment team visits the laboratory. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00174 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Planning is the means by which the lead assessor identifies 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
all the required activities to be completed during the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessment process. These activities include obtaining 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
records before the assessment, conducting the assessment, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
writing reports and following up. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Pre-assessment activities include: deciding the scope of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessment (Section 3.4.2); assessment planning (Section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
3.4.3); reviewing NELAP/State information (Section 3.4.4); 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
providing advance notification of the assessment to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratory (Section 3.4.5); coordinating the assessment team 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(Section 3.4.6); and gathering assessment documents and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
equipment (Section 3.4.7). Section 3.4.8 discusses 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Confidential Business Information issues. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.4.2&blank;Scope of the Assessment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The first step in the assessment planning process is deciding 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
what type of assessment will be conducted. The assessments 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
usually include a laboratory evaluation and a records review. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.4.2.1&blank;Laboratory Evaluations 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
A laboratory assessment obtains a ``snapshot in time'' at a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
testing laboratory by evaluating what activities are being 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
conducted when the assessment takes place. During a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratory evaluation, the assessment team may identify a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
number of samples or a recently completed or on-going project 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and evaluate to what extent the tests are being conducted 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
according to NELAP or client requirements. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.4.2.2&blank;Records Review 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The purpose of a records review is to learn if the testing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratory has maintained data and other information necessary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to support reports previously issued. During a records 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
review, team members will conduct an overall audit of data, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and will compare data with submitted reports to determine 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
whether the data were generated or collected following the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proper procedures in the NELAP/State, EPA, or client 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.4.3&blank;Assessment Planning 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Planning includes conducting a thorough review, prior to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessment, of NELAP and/or State records pertaining to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratory to be inspected. This will save time because 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
familiarity with the operation, history, and compliance status 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the laboratory increases the efficiency and focus of an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on-site visit. Planning also promotes a better relationship 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
with the laboratory community because the lead assessor will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be better able to answer questions concerning the application 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of NELAP/State requirements to a particular laboratory. It also 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enhances the laboratory's confidence in the lead assessor and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
aids in establishing good relationships with laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
representatives. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Another important benefit of planning is to enhance the lead 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessor's ability to identify and document potential problems 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and plan to collect necessary information to assist the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accrediting authority in their subsequent decisions concerning 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the laboratory. Planning an assessment will result in an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
efficient and productive assessment overall. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.4.4&blank;Reviewing NELAP/State Information 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The lead assessor's responsibilities start with receipt of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Assessment Assignment. For a records review, copies of all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
appropriate documents related to the laboratory will be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
forwarded by the accrediting authority to the lead assessor or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
directly to a team member, if appropriate, ideally at least 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
six weeks prior to the start of the assessment. The lead 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessor should request any other information that will be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
useful in preparing for the assessment. Such information may 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
include: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00175 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(a) Copies of previous assessment reports and PE sample 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
results; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) General laboratory information such as laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
submitted self-assessment forms, SOPs and Quality 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Assurance plan; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) Correspondence with laboratory personnel; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(d) Discussion with appropriate NELAP/State staff; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(e) Available documents from recipients of reports from the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratory; and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(f) Relevant program documents such as NELAP/State 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
guidelines or SOPs. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.4.5&blank;Providing Advance Notification 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
No fewer than two weeks prior to an announced assessment, the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accrediting body will contact the responsible management 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
official at the laboratory to schedule the assessment. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
initial telephone notification will be confirmed by a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
notification letter. A copy of the notification letter also 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
will be given to the lead assessor. An assessment assignment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that gives the name and telephone number of the laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
contact person and of each assessment team member, as well as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
other available information necessary to the planning and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
conduct of the assessment will also be provided to the lead 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessor. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Once the laboratory has been notified by the accrediting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
authority that an assessment will be conducted, the primary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
responsibility for the conduct of the assessment passes to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
lead assessor. Any further communications with the laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
personnel should be made by the lead assessor. The lead 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessor should keep his/her supervisory personnel informed of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the status of the assessment, and should consult with them on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
any substantive problems that may arise or changes that may be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
required. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
There are several items to be addressed in the advanced 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
notification. The lead assessor should make note of when and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to whom advance notification was provided. Written advance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
notification should do the following: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) Introduce the lead assessor and team members to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratory; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) Schedule the assessment, including establishing time of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
arrival; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) Obtain verbal agreement for entry; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(d) Confirm the appropriate address for the assessment, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
including identifying the location of necessary records, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
as specified in the assessment plan; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(e) Ensure that laboratory personnel are available to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accompany assessors during the assessment; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(f) Encourage the laboratory to transfer all records to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessment site before the assessment; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(g) Obtain directions to the laboratory; and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(h) Allow discussion of problems, concerns, or questions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
about the assessment or any other issues. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Especially when the laboratory has not previously been 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessed by the accrediting authority, the lead assessor 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
should be certain that laboratory personnel are aware of what 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
an assessment involves, what data and records should be made 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
available and what personnel should be present. If the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratory representative does not cooperate, the lead 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessor's supervisor and the accrediting authority management 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
should be consulted for instructions on how to proceed. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00176 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.4.6&blank;Assessment Team Coordination 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
When the identity of the assessment team is known, the lead 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessor should contact each person and begin planning the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
conduct of the assessment. As early as possible the lead 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessor should: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) Coordinate travel plans, including the hotel and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
transportation arrangements; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) Notify each team member of the dates of the assessment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and pre-assessment team meeting; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) Ensure that each team member has been briefed on specific 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
procedures for the assessment; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(d) Define the time allotted for the assessment. The lead 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessor should be careful not to underestimate the time 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
needed to conduct the assessment; and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(e) Confirm for those individuals who will be conducting the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
records review, their familiarity with the records to be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reviewed. Each member of the assessment team should be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
aware of their responsibilities during the assessment. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The lead assessor should also arrange to provide copies of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
applicable NELAP/State standard operating procedures (SOPs) to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
team members who do not already possess these documents. In 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
addition, the lead assessor may need to assure that the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessment team is aware of proper procedures for receipt and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
handling of confidential business information (CBI). The lead 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessor should determine the level of experience of each team 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
member in conducting laboratory evaluations or records reviews 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
under NELAP/State requirements. The lead assessor may need 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to guide less experienced team members, both prior to and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
during the assessment as well as with report preparation. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
lead assessor should assemble the team just prior to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessment to attend to last minute details. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.4.7&blank;Gathering Assessment Documents and Equipment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Besides preparing the assessment plan and reviewing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accrediting body records and laboratory submissions prior to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
conducting the assessment, the lead assessor should gather and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prepare the necessary documents and equipment to be used 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
during the assessment. No single list of documents and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
equipment can be appropriate for all assessments. The lead 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessor's experience in the field and information obtained 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
during pre-assessment planning should assist in preparing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
lists tailored to specific assessment sites and needs. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Specific needs will be determined by the requirements of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessment, the availability of equipment, conditions at the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratory, NELAP/State policies, and whether advance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
notification of an assessment is given. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.4.7.1&blank;Types of Documents 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Documents necessary for the assessment should be prepared 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
before the assessment, whenever possible. The lead assessor 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
should obtain copies of the required assessment forms. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Several spare copies of each form should always be carried. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Assessments may require:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 02 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=15 g=1 f=1 -->
_Notice of assessment; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Assessment confidentiality notice; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Conflict of interest form; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Assessor credentials; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Assessment assignment; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Assessment notification letter; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Attendance sheet, opening and closing conference; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_assessment appraisal form.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 02 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
In addition, the lead assessor should be certain to take the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
following documents and materials on an assessment: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00177 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(a) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Copies of NELAP/State requirements.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 Lead assessors 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
should have copies of the applicable NELAP/State 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements available upon request. Having such data 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
available can help improve the relationship between 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
NELAP/State and the laboratory community, which can 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
foster better laboratory compliance; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
NELAP/State checklists
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 for evaluations; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
NELAP/State outreach materials.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 Lead assessors should 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provide current, relevant educational, and/or guidance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
information to laboratory officials upon request or as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
deemed appropriate by the lead assessor; and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(d) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Administrative information.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 Travel authorizations and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
telephone numbers of travel and procurement personnel who 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
may need to be contacted should be taken by the lead 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessor when on travel. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.4.7.2&blank;Assessment Equipment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The types of equipment that a lead assessor takes to an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessment site will vary from assessment to assessment, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
depending upon the nature and extent of the assessment and the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
type of testing laboratory to be inspected. Therefore, prior 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to each assessment, the lead assessor should check the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
equipment to make sure that it is in good working condition. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Since each assessment is unique, no single list of equipment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or forms can be devised that will fit every assessment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
situation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.4.8&blank;Confidential Business Information Considerations 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
NELAP/State SOPs protect Confidential Business Information 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(CBI) from disclosure. CBI includes trade secrets (including 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
process, formulation, or production data) and certain 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
financial information, the uncontrolled disclosure of which 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
could cause damage to a laboratory's competitive position. In 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
general, disclosure of CBI is prohibited, except in certain 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
limited situations. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The lead assessor should keep in mind that information 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
obtained from a laboratory during an assessment can, for the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
most part, be disclosed in response to a request from the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
public, or other requesting party, under Federal or State 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Freedom of Information requirements. However, if the data has 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
been properly claimed as CBI, it may not generally be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
disclosed under these requirements. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
A lead assessor must present notice to laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
representatives of their right to claim data at the laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
as CBI and such claims are frequently made. Because the lead 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessor is very likely to require access to CBI before (i.e., 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
while preparing for an assessment), during, and after an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessment, the lead assessor must be knowledgeable of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
NELAP/State procedures governing access to, handling of, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
disclosure of CBI. The lead assessor and others who may use 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the information must have CBI access authorization, since only 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
authorized individuals may have access to CBI. A CBI-cleared 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
lead assessor may obtain access to CBI documents from the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accrediting authority by requesting access to the information 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
from the appropriate official. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Whether or not it is anticipated that CBI documents will be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
collected during an assessment, the lead assessor must provide 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a NELAP/State assessment confidentiality notice to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
responsible laboratory official at the beginning of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessment. This notice informs laboratory officials of their 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
right to claim part of the assessment data as CBI. The lead 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessor should be familiar with the procedures for asserting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a CBI claim, and the criteria that the claimed information 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
must meet. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The lead assessor must take custody of all CBI documents 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
before leaving the laboratory, and must maintain them in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
custody, using all proper procedures and safeguards, until 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
they can be received by the accrediting authority. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
3.5.&blank;Assessment Schedule/Format 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
