<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00178 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.5.1&blank;Length of Evaluation
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The length of an on-site assessment will depend upon a number of factors, such as the number of tests evaluated, the
number of assessors available, the size of the laboratory, the number of problems encountered during the assessment,
and the cooperativeness of the laboratory staff. The accrediting body should assign an adequate number of assessors
to complete the evaluation within a reasonable period of time. Assessors must strike a balance between thoroughness
and practicality, assuring that the assessment covers all aspects of the laboratory operation.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.5.2&blank;Opening Conference
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Arrival at the facility should occur during normal working hours. The facility representative should be located
as soon as the assessment team arrives on the premises. A laboratory's refusal to admit the assessment team for an evaluation
may result in an automatic failure or loss of accreditation on the part of the laboratory, unless there are extenuating
circumstances that are accepted by the accreditation body. The team leader should notify the accrediting body as
soon as possible after refusal of entry.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
When the appropriate official has been located, the team leader should introduce the team and should present credentials.
Many companies require that the assessment team sign a visitor's sheet that contains the name, time, reason for visit,
organization, etc., which should be signed. However, any request for any assessment team member to sign a ``visitor's
release'' or ``waiver'' that would relieve the company of responsibility for injury or that would limit the rights
of the accrediting body to use the data obtained should not be signed. If such a waiver or release is presented, the team
leader should politely explain that they cannot sign and request a blank sign-in sheet. The assessment team leader
should brief the appropriate responsible official(s) of the facility to introduce team members, explain areas to
be evaluated and verify application information.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The assessment team leader should request relevant documents for review that were not part of the application materials,
such as standard operating procedures, chain-of-custody forms, report forms, etc.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The assessment appraisal form should be presented to the appropriate laboratory official with a request that the
form be completed and returned to the accrediting authority after the assessment. This form will allow feedback from
the laboratory on the manner in which the assessment was conducted.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.5.3&blank;Records Review
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The records requested during the opening conference will be reviewed by assessment team members for accuracy, completeness
and proper methodology for each area to be evaluated.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Trade secrets and confidential business information are protected from public disclosure. The type of information
that may be considered confidential business information is defined in 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Title 40, Code of Federal Regulation, Part 2.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 All financial and trade information should be kept confidential, if so requested by the laboratory. All other information
for all aspects of application, assessment and accreditation of laboratories is considered public information.
If the laboratory requests that information other than that noted above is confidential, the information should
be treated as confidential until a ruling can be made by the accreditation body.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The team leader must mark all confidential information received and handle it as required by appropriate laws and
regulations.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00179 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.5.4&blank;Staff Interviews
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The assessment team will evaluate a test by having the individual that normally conducts the specific procedure walk
through the procedure, including a step-by-step description of exactly what is done and what equipment and supplies
are employed. The assessor will note and record the procedure on the standardized checklists for that particular
test and application. Any deficiencies shall also be noted and discussed with the individual.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The assessment team members shall have the authority to conduct interviews with any/all staff and, if necessary,
conduct private interviews. Calculations, data transfers, calibration procedures, quality control/assurance
practices and adherence to SOP's shall be assessed for each test.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
During the evaluation, sufficient information may become available to indicate that a particular person has violated
an environmental law or regulation, such as knowingly making a false statement on a report. This information should
be carefully documented, since it may be used in a legal action. When the possibility of additional legal investigation
exists, the assessor should not discuss the legal implications of the suspected violation with the individual or
any laboratory representative. However, the assessor should continue to gather the information necessary to complete
the accreditation assessment.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.5.5&blank;Closing Conference
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The assessment team should meet with representatives following the evaluation of the laboratory for an informal
debriefing and discussion of findings.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In the event the laboratory disagrees with the findings of the assessor(s), and the team leader adheres to the original
findings, the area(s) protested shall be documented by the team leader and included in the report to the accreditation
body for consideration. The accrediting authority will make the final determination.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The assessment team should provide the accreditation body with an assessment report encompassing all relevant information
concerning the ability of the applicant laboratory to comply with the accreditation requirements. If data is available
from performance evaluation testing, this should be included in the final report.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.5.6&blank;Follow-Up Procedures
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The accrediting authority will issue the assessment to the applicant laboratory that outlines any areas of deficiencies.
The applicant laboratory should then submit a plan of corrective action, if necessary, and provide any missing documentation
required within 45 days from the date of report receipt.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
After reviewing documentation and corrective actions, the accrediting authority will make the decision to pass,
fail or provide interim accreditation for a laboratory. If the deficiencies listed are substantial or numerous,
an additional assessment (possibly unannounced) may be conducted before a final decision for accreditation can
be made.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
3.6&blank;Criteria for Assessment
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.6.1&blank;Assessor's Manual
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The NELAC will develop a manual(s) for on-site assessors to assure that on-site assessments are performed in a uniform,
consistent manner. The manual(s) will be provided when assessors take the NELAC required training (section 3.2.1)
and will serve as guidance for on-site assessment personnel.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The manual(s) provided to on-site assessors should include instructions for evaluating the following items:
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00180 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(a) Size, appearance, adequacy of the laboratory facility;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) Organization and management of the laboratory;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) Qualifications and experience of laboratory personnel;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(d) Receipt, tracking and handling of samples;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(e) Quantity, condition, performance of laboratory instrumentation and equipment;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(f) Preparation and traceability of calibration standards;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(g) Analytical and biological methodology (including the laboratory's standard operating procedures as well as
confirmation of individuals' adherence to SOPs, and the individual's proficiency with the methodology);
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(h) Data reduction procedures, including an examination of raw data and confirmation that final reported results
can be traced to the raw data/original observations;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(i) Quality assurance/quality control procedures, including adherence to the laboratory's quality assurance
plan and adequacy of the plan;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(j) General health and safety procedures as they relate to good scientific practices;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(k) Laboratory waste disposal procedures;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(l) Environmental and toxicological test methods and SOPs; and
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(m) Care, use, and maintenance of test organisms.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.6.2&blank;Assessors Role
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
When performing an on-site laboratory evaluation, the assessor must appraise each of the areas listed in section
3.6.1. The on-site assessor should use a variety of tools in the evaluation process. The experience of the assessor,
his/her observations, interviews with laboratory staff, and examination of SOPs, raw data, and the laboratory's
documentation will all play an important role in the assessment. The role of the on-site assessor is a critical one
in the entire laboratory accreditation process. The accreditation of a particular laboratory will depend to a large
extent on the assessor's recommendation. While much of the on-site assessment will depend upon the assessor's judgement,
the recommendation not to accredit a laboratory must be based on factual information, not on opinions or suppositions.
Therefore it is crucial that the on-site assessor have a clear understanding of the laboratory's procedures and policies,
and that the assessor document any deficiencies. Also the assessor should discuss any deficiencies with the laboratory's
management in order to allow them to provide additional information which might affect the assessor's recommendations.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.6.3&blank;Checklists
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Standardized checklists for the on-site assessment must be used. The use of checklists does not discourage the need
for additional observations and staff interviews, but is merely another tool in the assessor's inventory which assists
in conducting a thorough and efficient evaluation. Using a checklist as a substitute for assessor training and experience
must not occur.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 04 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=4 -->
Note: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
It is anticipated that standardized checklists will be developed or adopted by NELAC's On-Site Assessment Committee
for the assessor's review of analytical and biological methodology.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.6.4&blank;Evaluation Criteria
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00181 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The following considerations should be taken into account by on-site assessors when evaluating the areas listed
in section 3.6.1:
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.6.4.1&blank;Facility Assessment
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The assessor(s) should tour the laboratory facility with the laboratory management representative. Usually the
tour will occur during the initial phase of the on-site visit, perhaps after the opening conference. During the tour,
the assessor should visually inspect the facility with respect to general housekeeping, cleanliness, lighting,
bench space and continuous temperature monitoring (if required). The assessor should note whether the appropriate
laboratory services (e.g., vacuum system, compressed air, gases, etc.) are available. It may be necessary to have
the laboratory representative demonstrate that certain pieces of equipment are working properly, for example,
a fume hood may be turned on to assure that it does indeed exhaust air from the laboratory. This type of demonstration
is not intended to certify that the hood meets design specifications or safety requirements, but merely that it is
operational. During the tour, the assessor(s) should determine if sample storage areas are sufficient and whether
there are problems with laboratory operations which would affect data quality. For example, an extraction operation
located in the same room where volatile organic analyses are performed could contribute contamination to the volatile
organic analyses. Any problems or deficiencies with the laboratory facility should be brought to the attention of
the laboratory management at the time of the tour and reinforced at the closing conference. If discrepancies are noted
between statements made by the laboratory representative and visual observations, it may be necessary to interview
other laboratory personnel to obtain an explanation of the situation. As with all areas of the on-site assessment,
the experience and training of the on-site assessor are critical to the success of the facilities evaluation.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.6.4.2&blank;Organization Assessment
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The assessor should review laboratory QA plans, SOPs, organizational charts and/or other documentation to determine
the laboratory's operational structure. If a documented organizational plan exists, the assessor should ascertain
during subsequent interviews with laboratory personnel if the laboratory operation follows the documented plan.
The assessor should interview laboratory management to determine the roles of management and how laboratory policy
is created. The absence of a documented organizational structure, clearly defined functional responsibilities,
and lines of communication, should be considered a deficiency.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.6.4.3&blank;Personnel Assessment
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The assessor should review the laboratory's written qualification requirements for each position, and the qualifications
of those persons currently holding the positions. Key personnel, e.g., laboratory management staff, quality assurance
coordinator, section managers, chief analysts, etc., should be interviewed to verify their qualifications for
their positions. These interviews may be conducted concurrently with interviews on analytical and biological procedures,
quality control requirements, etc., in order to expedite the process. The assessor should be cautious when making
judgments on personnel qualifications, and must be aware that experience may be an acceptable substitute for formal
education. When in doubt concerning personnel qualifications, the assessor should conduct an in-depth interview
with the individual to determine his/her expertise in a given area.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 04 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=4 -->
Note: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
Section 5, Quality Systems, contains details on personnel qualifications.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.6.4.4&blank;Sample Handling Assessment
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00182 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The assessor should review the laboratory's SOP for sample 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
receipt to assure that all appropriate elements (e.g., proper 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sample containers, preservatives, chain of custody, sample 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
storage, sample rejection policy, etc.) are included. Any 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
omissions should be brought to the attention of the laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
management and appropriate laboratory staff person. Absence 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of a written sample receipt SOP should be considered a serious 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
deficiency. The assessor should inspect the sample storage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
areas to insure that the facilities are adequate and secured. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Cold storage facilities should be checked for maintenance of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proper temperatures, proper monitoring devices (thermometers, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
etc.) and appropriate documentation. Sample receipt personnel 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
should be interviewed to determine their adherence to the SOP. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Sample receipt documentation and chain-of-custody records 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
should be reviewed to determine if documentation is adequate. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Failure to follow SOPs may be considered a serious deficiency, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
depending on the degree of deviation. Failure to keep sample 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
receipt and chain-of-custody documentation should be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
considered a serious deficiency. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.6.4.5&blank;Equipment Assessment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The assessor should determine if the laboratory has all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
equipment and instrumentation necessary to perform the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
analyses for which certification is requested. This 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
determination should be performed by visual inspection of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratory. The assessor should determine if the equipment is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in reasonable working condition. An actual demonstration of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
equipment performance is not necessary in all circumstances, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
but should be required if the assessor has doubts about the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
condition of certain pieces of equipment. The absence of a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
required piece of equipment or instrument for a particular 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
test should be considered a serious deficiency. The assessor 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
should determine if the laboratory has written records of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
equipment repairs, maintenance, testing and calibration. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.6.4.6&blank;Calibration Standards Assessment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The assessor shall ascertain whether the laboratory has the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
necessary stock calibration standards and should spot check 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
calibration standards to see if they are within expiration 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
dates. The assessor should determine if stock standards are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
properly stored, e.g., volatile organic standards are stored 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in sealed vials in a freezer. The assessor should examine the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratory's records for stock standards and the preparation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of working standards to determine if the records are complete. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.6.4.7&blank;Methodology Assessment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The assessor should determine whether the laboratory has 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standard operating procedures for all test methods used by the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratory. The standard operating procedures should be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reviewed to determine if they adequately address all aspects 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the analytical and biological procedures, e.g., sample 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
preparation, calibration standard preparation, instrument 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
calibration, etc. The analysts should be interviewed to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
verify that they have access to and are following the standard 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operating procedures for all methods. The lack of analytical 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and biological standard operating procedures or significant 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
deviations from the standard operating procedures should be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
considered as serious deficiencies. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
While the ideal on-site assessment would consist, in part, of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
observing each individual perform his/her assigned work, time 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
considerations will not permit this approach in a laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which conducts a wide variety of analytical or biological 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
procedures. Consequently, the on-site assessor will need to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rely more heavily on interviews with laboratory personnel, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
observations, and review of records to determine proficiency 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
with, and knowledge of, the analytical or biological 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
methodology. The assessor's experience and training will play 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a key role in this process. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The assessor should be familiar with the performance of a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
test, so that the appropriate technical questions may be asked 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the laboratory's analysts. The assessor should pose 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
questions to the laboratory's staff in such a way as to not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
lead the individual into the correct response. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
individual's responses should be cross-checked with the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratory's documentation. During interviews with the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
individuals, it may be unclear as to how the analytical and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
biological procedures are being performed. If this occurs, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
then the assessor should ask the individual to demonstrate the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
procedure. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.6.4.8&blank;Data Audit 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The assessor should perform a data audit on an appropriate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
number of sample sets which contain all the tests for which 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the laboratory is seeking accreditation. It may be necessary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to audit multiple sample sets in order to cover all tests. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The assessor should verify that the required sample receipt 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
documentation and chain-of-custody records are on file and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that they contain all necessary information. The assessor 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
should obtain final data reports for the sample set being 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
audited. The assessor should verify that the final reports 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
contain the following information: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 02 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=15 g=1 f=1 -->
_Sample receipt date; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Sample analysis date; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Sample identification; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Method used for analysis; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Quantitation units, e.g., mg/L, mg/Kg, &mu;g/m
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=15 g=5 f=1 -->
3
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=15 g=1 f=1 -->
, etc.; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00183 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=15 g=1 f=1 -->
_If sample is a solid, whether results are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
calculated on a wet weight or dry weight basis, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
if on a dry weight basis, the percent moisture or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
percent solids; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_The sample result (if the result is none detected, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the method detection limit should also be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reported); and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Method of statistical determination of test result, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
if applicable. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 02 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The assessor should assure that all information needed to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
verify the final result is on file, including reasons for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
invalidating testing results if this has occurred. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
information may include sample preparation data, instrument 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
output (chromatograms, mass spectra, strip charts), instrument 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
calibration records, and records of dilutions. Once the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
information is located, the assessor should recreate the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
calculation in order to verify the final reported result. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
absence of the required information needed to verify the final 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
result should be considered a serious deficiency. If the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessor is unable to recreate a calculation, the problem 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
should be discussed with laboratory personnel in an attempt to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
resolve the issue. If any calculations/final results are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
determined to be incorrect, the assessor should examine 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
approximately ten percent of the data for the test in question 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
over a selected time period to see if a systematic error has 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
occurred. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In addition to auditing results from routine sample analyses, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessors must also audit results of performance evaluation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(PE) samples analyzed by the laboratory for the NELAP. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Assessors should verify that the sample(s) were analyzed using 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the criteria set forth by NELAP. The data generated during 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the analysis of PE samples should be examined and compared 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
with final results reported to the NELAP. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.6.4.9&blank;QA Plan Assessment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The assessor should examine the laboratory's written QA Plan 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to determine if it conforms to the Quality Systems 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements in Section 5.0. The assessor should examine the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratory's raw data to ascertain if the required QC checks 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
have been documented. If QC criteria were exceeded, the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessor must determine if corrective action was initiated. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Laboratory personnel should be interviewed to determine if 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
they understand and follow the requirements of the QA Plan. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Laboratory management should be interviewed to determine their 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
commitment to the QA program. The absence of a QA Plan, or an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
incomplete QA Plan, should be considered a major deficiency. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The lack of appropriate corrective action or documentation of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
corrective action should be considered a serious deficiency. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00184 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.6.4.10&blank;General Health and Safety Procedures 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The responsibility for promulgating and enforcing occupational 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
safety and health standards rests with the U.S. Department of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Labor.
<FOOTCITE>1</FOOTCITE>
 While it is not within the scope of the assessment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
team to evaluate all health and safety regulations, any 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
obviously unsafe condition(s) should be described to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
appropriate laboratory official, and reported to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
appropriate state or federal agency. The accreditation on-
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
site assessment is not intended to certify that the laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is in compliance with all applicable health and safety 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulations. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
1
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=3 -->
Handbook for Analytical Quality Control in Water and Wastewater Laboratories
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
, EPA&hyph;600/4&hyph;70&hyph;019, March 1979.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.6.4.11&blank;Laboratory Waste Disposal Assessment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The assessor(s) should ask if adequate facilities are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
available for the collection, storage and/or treatment (if 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
applicable) of all laboratory wastes. The waste disposal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
system(s) should be operated in such a manner to protect the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
air, water, and land by minimizing and controlling all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
releases from fume hoods and bench operations. Compliance is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
also required with any wastewater discharge permits and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulations. It is the laboratory's responsibility to comply 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
with all federal, state, and local regulations governing waste 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
management, particularly the hazardous waste regulations. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The accreditation on-site assessment is not intended to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
certify that the laboratory is in compliance with all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
applicable waste disposal regulations. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
3.7&blank;Documentation of On-Site Assessment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.7.1&blank;Checklists 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The checklists used by the assessors during the assessment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
should become a part of the permanent file kept by the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
NELAP/State on each laboratory. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.7.2&blank;Report Format 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Evaluation reports should be generated in a narrative format, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
allowing for differences in style and technique between 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accrediting authorities. Deficiencies must be addressed at a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
minimum, however, documentation of positive aspects should be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
included. Documentation of existing conditions at the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratory should be included in each report to serve as a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
baseline for future contacts with the facility. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.7.3&blank;Distribution 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The accrediting authority should be recognized as having the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
responsibility for the content of the evaluation reports. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
team leader should compile, edit and submit the final report 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to the accrediting authority. The team leader must assure 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that the results within the final report conform to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
established criteria for the evaluated parameters. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.7.4&blank;Report Deadline 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
No longer than thirty (30) days should elapse from the last 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
day of an on-site evaluation until the report is submitted to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the accrediting authority for review and final decision. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.7.5&blank;Release of Report 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
On-site evaluation reports should be released by the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accrediting authority only. The reports will be released to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the management of the affected laboratory and to those persons 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nominated by the laboratory to receive a copy of the report. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The assessment report shall not be released until the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessment and all other appropriate action has been 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
completed. In accordance with the Freedom of Information 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements, any documentation adjudged to be proprietary, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
financial and/or trade information will be considered exempt 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
from release to the public. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3.7.6&blank;Report Storage Time 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
At a minimum, copies of all evaluation reports must be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
retained by the evaluators and the accrediting authority for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a period of five years, or longer if required by regulation.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00185 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=84 g=1 f=1 -->
4.0&blank;Accreditation Process 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
4.1&blank;Components of Accreditation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
These criteria must be fulfilled for accreditation. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
components and criteria are herein described. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
4.1.1&blank;Personnel Qualifications 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
This component ensures that the managerial and supervisory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
personnel in the environmental laboratory meet a minimum set 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of qualifications that address the elements of education, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
training and experience. It should be recognized that some of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
these elements are interconnectable, i.e. a greater magnitude 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of training and/or experience may substitute for lesser 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
degrees of formal education. Refer to Quality Systems for a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
detailed review of supervisors and managers, and the criteria 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to be maintained by the supervisors and managers for awarding 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accreditation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
4.1.2&blank;On-Site Assessments 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
On-site assessments and evaluations may be of two types: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
announced and unannounced. The assessment ensures that the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
environmental laboratory is capable of performing analyses to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the level, precision and accuracy required by the specific 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
method or performance based method. Announced assessments test 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
these methods and evaluate the results against the criteria 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
under the best circumstances in a controlled environment. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
unannounced assessment measures the abilities of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratory to meet these standards for methods on an average 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
day under normal working conditions and in a normal working 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
environment. Each type of assessment has limitations and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
advantages, but the information obtained from both will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provide a higher degree of confidence in the ability of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratory to attain a required level of competence in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
quality of data produced for regulatory and compliance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
purposes. Refer to on-site assessment for additional 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
information regarding frequency, procedures, criteria, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
scheduling and documentation of on-site assessments. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Announced Assessments
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
_The elements present in and criteria 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for announced assessments for national accreditation are: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) The assessment must be performed a minimum of one time 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
per year and be conducted on-site; i.e., the site at 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which the actual analyses take place; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) The assessment may consist of any or all of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
categories for which the laboratory wants to obtain 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accreditation; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) The inspector must have access to all information and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
data requested both for analyses completed and laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
personnel; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(d) The results of the assessment and the Performance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Evaluation sample analyses indicating satisfactory or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
unsatisfactory performance will be sent to the National 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Database on environmental laboratories; and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(e) At least two performance evaluation (PE) samples, twice 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
per year, for each method or field of testing, must be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
successfully analyzed according to the standards 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
established for quality assurance/quality control, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
precision and accuracy. It may not be required to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
analyze PE samples during the on-site assessment. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Marginal performance on any previous PE samples can be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
grounds for requiring that a subsequent PE sample 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
analysis be performed under the observation of an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inspector. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Unannounced Assessments
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
_The elements and criteria for the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
unannounced assessments for the purpose of the national 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accreditation program are: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) The inspector may not be denied immediate access to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratory facility; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00186 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(b) Elements (a) through (d) under announced assessments are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
also applicable to unannounced assessments; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) Performance evaluation samples may be distributed and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
analyses run in the categories and for the methods that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are determined by and prescribed by the inspector; and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(d) All performance evaluation samples and other analyses 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
required by the inspector are to be done as directed by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the inspector. These include parameters such as: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
specified equipment, analysts and times, but are not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
limited to these factors. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Factors Examined in Announced and Unannounced Laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Assessments.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
_Refer to On-site Assessments for assessment criteria required 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to be satisfied for accreditation. It should be noted, the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inspector is not limited to these factors in reaching an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
evaluation and conclusion. Other factors may be considered 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and documented as appropriate. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Laboratories will be furnished with an inspection report 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
documenting any deficiencies found in the factors listed above 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or any others considered by the inspector. It shall also 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
include whether a specific method passed or failed based on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Performance Evaluation sample. All such reports are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
public record and any or all of the information contained 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
therein may be put into the National Database. Proprietary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
data will be excepted from all public records. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The laboratory will have no more than 45 days from the date of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
receipt of the report to correct deficiencies noted in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inspection report. At that time, if no remedial action has 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
been taken to correct the noted deficiencies, accreditation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for categories or specific methods within those categories 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
will be immediately revoked. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
4.1.3&blank;Performance Evaluation Samples 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
A critical component of laboratory assessments is the analysis 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the Performance Evaluation Samples. Refer to Performance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Evaluation Testing, specifically Testing of Samples, for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
additional information regarding separate treatment of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Performance Evaluation samples, discussion of issues of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
availability, and purity and distribution. Performance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Evaluation samples would be used and evaluated in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accreditation process in the following manner: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) All laboratories seeking National Accreditation must 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
receive, examine and analyze initial performance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
evaluation sample(s) for each category (e.g., drinking 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
water, hazardous waste, etc.) in which they are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requesting accreditation. The analysis must be completed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and the results reported to the performance evaluation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
testing organization or the Inspector within 45 days of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the receipt of the sample. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) Each laboratory seeking national accreditation shall also 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be required to perform analyses on at least two 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
performance evaluation samples, two concentrations, two 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
times per year in each category for which they have 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
applied for accreditation or for which the laboratory is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
currently accredited. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) The laboratory will be informed of the results of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
performance evaluation sample analysis within 60 days of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
receipt by the state agency or authorized third party 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
contractor. The results of all of the performance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
evaluation sample tests indicating satisfactory or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
unsatisfactory compliance will be public record and will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be recorded on the national database. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(d) The results of the performance evaluation sample analysis 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
will be considered, along with other information obtained 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
from announced and/or unannounced assessments in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
determining whether accreditation should be granted, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
denied or modified for a category, or whether the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratory should lose accreditation for a category or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
method within a category. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
4.1.4&blank;Corrective Action Reports 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The purpose of the corrective action report is to have a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
written record of response to deficiencies that are noted in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the laboratory assessment procedure. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) After being notified of deficiencies from the laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inspection, the laboratory has 45 days from the date of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
receipt of the deficiency report to submit a corrective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
action report. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) The state authority or authorized third party contractor 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
will respond to the action noted in the corrective action 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
report within 30 days of receiving it. The report must 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
address each of the deficiencies noted on the deficiency 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
report. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) A laboratory can lose accreditation in a category or a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
method within a category by any or all of the following 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
items: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
i. Failing to respond to corrective action two times; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ii. Failing to submit a corrective action report; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00187 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
iii. Failing to address each item noted as a deficiency 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in the corrective action report; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
iv. Failing the same performance evaluation sample 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
analysis two consecutive times for the same 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
analyte; or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
v. Failing to achieve an overall testing event passing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
score for two consecutive testing events or two out 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of three consecutive testing events. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(d) All information included and documented in a deficiency 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
report and the corrective action report are considered to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be public information. Other states participating in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
National Environmental Laboratory Accreditation Program 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would have access to this information through a national 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
database. At a minimum, the database would include the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
following information: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
i. Name and location of laboratory; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ii. Number and dates of assessments performed and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
whether they were announced or unannounced; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
iii. Performance evaluation samples and analyses done, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the date completed and the status (in process; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
passed, failed); 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
iv. Categories and methods for which the laboratory is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
currently accredited and date of accreditation; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and/or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
v. Categories and method for which the laboratory has 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
lost accreditation and the date of loss of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accreditation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
4.1.5&blank;Ethical Standards 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Elements in a national program that ensure consistency and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
promote the use of quality assurance/quality control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
procedures to generate quality data for regulatory purposes 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) NELAC strongly recommends requires that each laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
seeking national accreditation have a named Quality 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Assurance Officer. NELAC strongly recommends that the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Quality Assurance Officer be a person other than any 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
supervisor of laboratory analysts, who reports directly 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to the laboratory management and not to the laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
supervisor in matters related to quality assurance and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
quality control of analyses, methods relating to these 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
analyses, and instrumentation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) NELAC will consider that responsibility for falsification 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of data, records or instrument parameters will rest upon 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Quality Assurance Officer (named in 4.1.4a above), 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the laboratory management and the company. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) The National Environmental Laboratory Accreditation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Program shall establish a ``Laboratory Fraud Hotline'' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
telephone number. Alleged cases of data, record or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
analytical fraud reported via this hotline will be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
referred to the relevant state authority for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
investigation. The fact that a federal or state has 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
taken regulatory, legal, or contractual action against a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratory will be made available on the national 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
database. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
4.1.6&blank;Fee Process for National Accreditation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Refer to Policy and Structure, specifically funding of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program 1.7.3, regarding the funding of state accreditation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
programs, including a fee structure covering the actual cost 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of an accreditation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) The cost incurred in the application process for national 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
environmental laboratory accreditation will be called an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accreditation fee. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) Where required, accreditation fees will be paid to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
state(s) which grants accreditation to the laboratory. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
These fees must be paid in accordance with existing state 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulations, levels and practices. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00188 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(c) Failure to remit the accreditation fee within the time 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
limit as established by the individual state authority 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
will be grounds for immediate loss of accreditation in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that state. The loss of accreditation will immediately 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be entered in the national database. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
4.1.7&blank;Application Process 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The National Environmental Laboratory Accreditation Program 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
encompasses a standardized set of elements in each application 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for accreditation that will be reported to and recorded in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
national database. The application package includes any 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
specific state regulatory requirements that are essential for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accreditation within an individual state. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The application form for national environmental laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accreditation shall include: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) Legal name of laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) Laboratory mailing address 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) Name of owner 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(d) Location (full address) of laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(e) Name and phone number of laboratory contact person 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(f) Name and phone number of Quality Assurance Officer 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(g) Name and phone number of laboratory management 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
representative 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(h) Laboratory hours of operation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(i) States for which the laboratory is requesting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accreditation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(j) Categories for which the laboratory is requesting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accreditation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(k) Description of laboratory type
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 02 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=15 g=1 f=1 -->
_Commercial 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Federal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Hospital or health care 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_State 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_University 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Public water system 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Public wastewater system 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Industrial (an industry with discharge permits) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Other (Describe)
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=15 g=7 f=2 -->
XXXXXX
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=15 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 02 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(1) Certification of compliance by laboratory management 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(vide infra: 4.1.9) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
4.1.8&blank;Transfer of Ownership/Change of Ownership and/or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Location of Laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Accreditation may be transferred when the legal status or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ownership of an accredited laboratory changes without 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
affecting its staff, equipment, and organization. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accrediting agency may charge a transfer fee and shall conduct 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
an on-site assessment to verify affects of such changes on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratory performance. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The following conditions apply to the change in ownership 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and/or the change in location of a laboratory that has 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
national accreditation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) Any change in ownership and/or location of an accredited 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratory must be reported in writing to the primary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
state(s) and the National Environmental Laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Accreditation Program within twenty business days of such 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a change taking effect. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) Such a change in ownership and/or location will not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
necessarily require reaccreditation or reapplication in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
any or all of the categories in which the laboratory is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
currently accredited. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) Change in ownership and/or location may require a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
mandatory on-site assessment with the elements of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessment being determined by the inspector. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(d) Any change in ownership must assure historical 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
traceability of the laboratory accreditation number(s). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00189 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(e) For a change in ownership, one of the following 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
conditions must be in effect: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
i. The previous (transferring) owner must agree in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
writing, before the transfer of ownership takes 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
place, to be responsible for any analyses, data and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reports generated up to the time of legal transfer 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of ownership; or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ii. The buyer (transferee) must agree in writing to be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
responsible for any analyses, data and reports 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
generated before the legal transfer of ownership 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
occurs. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
4.1.9&blank;``Certification of Compliance'' Statement 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The following ``Certification of Compliance'' statement must 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accompany the application for laboratory accreditation. It 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
must be signed and dated by both the laboratory management and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the quality assurance officer for that laboratory. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=84 g=1 f=1 -->
Certification By Applicant 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The applicant understands and acknowledges that the laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is required to be continually in compliance with the National 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Environmental Laboratory Accreditation Program's rules and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulations concerning laboratory accreditation and standards 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and will be subject to the penalty provisions provided 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
therein. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The applicant understands and acknowledges that accreditation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is specifically subject to unannounced assessments. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Authorized representatives of any state in which the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratory is accredited may make an announced or unannounced 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inspection, search, or examination of an accredited or interim 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
approved laboratory whenever the state, at its discretion, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
considers such an inspection, search or examination necessary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to determine the extent of the laboratory's compliance with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the conditions of its accreditation and these regulations. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Any refusal to allow entry to the state's representatives 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
shall constitute a violation of a condition of accreditation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and grounds for revocation of accreditation or loss of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accreditation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The applicant hereby certifies that all analyses performed are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
done in accordance with applicable U.S. Environmental 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Protection Agency Guidelines. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
I hereby certify that I am authorized to sign this application 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on behalf of the applicant/owner and that there are no 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
misrepresentations in my answer to the questions on this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
application. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=77 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=10 g=1 f=1 -->
Signature Quality Assurance Officer 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Officer
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 02 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=77 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=10 g=1 f=1 -->
Name of Quality Assurance
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 02 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=77 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=10 g=1 f=1 -->
Print Name of Applicant Laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(Legal Name)
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 02 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=77 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=10 g=1 f=1 -->
Date
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 02 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

