<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
4.3.1&blank;Performance Evaluation Samples 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Performance evaluation samples appropriate for the accredited 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
methodology shall be acquired twice per year from a source 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
acceptable to the National Environmental Laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Accreditation Program, successfully analyzed, and reported to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the accrediting body within required deadlines. In the event 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of unsatisfactory performance and required reanalysis, repeat 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
analysis shall also be completed and reported within 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
established deadlines. Poor performance on a performance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
evaluation sample or failure to submit results within required 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
deadlines may be cause for downgrading accreditation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
4.3.2&blank;On-Site Assessments 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Announced on-site assessments shall be performed by the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accrediting agency at a minimum frequency of one assessment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
every year. Unannounced on-site assessments or follow-up on-site assessments may be conducted more frequently,
for cause, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
at the option of the accrediting agency. Situations which 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
might trigger an unannounced on-site assessment or follow-up 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on-site assessment include, review of a previously deficient 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on-site assessment, poor performance on a performance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
evaluation sample, change in other accreditation elements, or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
other information concerning the capabilities or practices of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the accredited laboratory. On-site assessments, regardless of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
frequency, shall be successfully completed to maintain 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accreditation. Deficiencies identified during the on-site 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessment shall be corrected within deadlines established in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
these guidelines or according to deadlines in an approved 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
correction action plan. Failure to pass an on-site assessment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or to correct deficiencies according to the provisions of an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
approved corrective action plan may be cause for downgrading 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accreditation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
4.3.3&blank;Other Accreditation Elements 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The accredited laboratory shall maintain other key 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accreditation elements which originally served as the basis 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for accreditation including the facility, organization and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
management, qualifications of key personnel, sample handling 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
procedures, calibration standards, analytical methods, data 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reduction procedures, and laboratory quality assurance plan. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Failure to maintain, revise, or replace any of these key 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
components may be cause for downgrading accreditation status. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
4.3.4&blank;Notification and Reporting Requirements 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The accredited laboratory shall notify the accrediting body of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
any changes in key accreditation criteria including but not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
necessarily limited to the laboratory ownership, location, key 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
personnel, and major instrumentation. The accredited lab shall 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
also comply with any other reporting requirements identified 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in these guidelines. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
4.3.5&blank;Record Keeping and Retention 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
All lab records associated with accreditation parameters, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
including raw data associated with each analysis, changes in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
method standard operating procedures, or the laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
quality assurance plan, shall be maintained for a minimum of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
five years unless otherwise designated for a longer period in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
another regulation. In the case of data used in litigation, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the laboratory is required to store such records for a longer 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
period upon written notification from the accrediting agency. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
4.3.6&blank;Payment of Fees 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The accredited lab shall pay all fees associated with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
maintaining accreditation to the accrediting body within 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
established deadlines. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
4.4&blank;Suspension, Revocation and Denial of Accreditation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00191 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Reasons to deny an initial application or reapplication shall 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
include: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) Failure of laboratory staff to meet the personnel 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
qualifications as required by NELAC. These 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
qualifications include education, training and experience 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) Failure to successfully perform performance evaluation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
test as required by NELAC. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) Failure to attest that analysis are performed by approved 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
methodologies and/or in accordance with NELAC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
A laboratory shall have two opportunities to correct the areas 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of deficiencies which results in a denial of applications. If 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the laboratory is not successful in remedying said 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
deficiencies, it must wait six months before again applying 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for accreditation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Revocation_shall mean the total withdrawal of a laboratory's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accreditation by the accrediting authority. The laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cannot reapply for accreditation for 6 months, by which time 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the reason/cause of the revocation must be corrected. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Reasons for revocation shall include: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) Failure to participate or unsatisfactory performance in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the performance evaluation testing program as required by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) Submitting performance evaluation sample results 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
generated by another laboratory. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) Misrepresentation of any material fact pertinent to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
receiving initial approval. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(d) Denial of entry for laboratory inspection. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(e) Conviction of charges of the falsification of any report 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of or relating to a laboratory analysis. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(f) Failure to pay accreditation fees. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
No laboratory's accreditation will be revoked or a renewal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
denied without the opportunity to request a hearing. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Suspension shall mean the temporary removal of a laboratory's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accreditation for a defined period of time. The purpose of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
suspension is to allow a laboratory time to correct 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
deficiencies or area of non-compliance with program 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements as defined by regulation. A suspended laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would not have to reapply for accreditation if the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cause/causes for suspension are corrected within six months. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
A laboratory's accreditation may be suspended in total or in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
part. It may retain those areas of accreditation where it 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
continues to meet the standards and requirements of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Reasons for suspension shall include: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) Failure to successfully perform performance evaluation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
tests pursuant to the requirements of the program; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) Failure to submit and implement corrective action related 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to deficiencies found during laboratory inspections; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) Loss of personnel with the required educational, training 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and experience qualifications; or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(d) Failure to pay accreditation application fees. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
4.5&blank;Interim Accreditation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
4.5.1&blank;Interim Accreditation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00192 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
If a laboratory completes all of the requirements for accreditation except that of an on-site assessment because
the accrediting authority is unable to schedule the assessment an interim accreditation shall be issued and will
be in effect until the assessment requirements have been completed. Interim accreditation will allow a laboratory
to perform analyses and report results of samples with the same status as a fully accredited laboratory until an on-site
assessment has been completed. Accreditation will still be granted when performance evaluation samples are not
available. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
4.5.2&blank;Revocation of Interim Accreditation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Revocation of interim accreditation may be initiated for due cause as described in 4.4.0 by order of the accrediting
agency, without right to a hearing. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
4.6&blank;Awarding of Accreditation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
When a participating laboratory has met the requirements specified for receiving accreditation, the laboratory
will receive a single certificate awarded on behalf of the state accrediting authority. The certificate will provide
the following information: the name of the laboratory, address of the laboratory, the specifications of the accreditation
action (for example, the laboratory may be accredited for analysis of water or for use of a specific analytical methodology,
etc.), the states in which the laboratory may operate. Even though a parent laboratory is accredited, the subfacilities
(laboratories operating under the same parent organization, analytical procedures, and quality assurance system)
are also required to become accredited. The subfacilities accredited will be listed on the certificate of the parent
laboratory. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
4.6.1&blank;The Certificate of Accreditation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The certificate of accreditation will briefly define the rules of obtaining and maintaining accreditation. Finally,
the certificate will be signed by a member of the accrediting authority. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
To address the concern that an individual state may revoke a laboratory's accreditation for work in that state, the
certificate will explain that continued accredited status depends on successful ongoing participation in the program.
The certificate will urge a customer to verify the laboratory's current accreditation standing within a particular
state. The certificate must be returned to the accrediting agency upon loss of accreditation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
4.6.2&blank;Changes in Areas of Accreditation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
If an accredited laboratory increases its areas of accreditation, a new certificate will be awarded which details
the spectrum of accreditations the laboratory has achieved. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
4.7&blank;Enforcement 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The development of an enforcement component of the National Environmental Laboratory Accreditation Program (NELAP)
should be based on explicit values, or principles, with which all participants concur. The proposed basic principles
are: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) The program should be fair to all participants; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) The rules should be well publicized; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) The program needs of the participating agencies must be upheld; and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(d) The due process rights of participating laboratories must be protected. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The major components of the program shall include: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) All enforcement actions are taken independently by EPA or state agencies and communicated to all other NELAP participating
agencies. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00193 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(b) NELAP enforcement is limited to suspension (short-to-long-term) from NELAP only. Any other civil/criminal
actions are taken by participating agencies. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) An effective information-sharing database used by all participating agencies is essential to ensure informed
decision-making based on lab performance. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
4.7.1&blank;Role of Enforcement vs QA/QC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Most agencies have historically conducted laboratory QA/QC programs designed to help laboratories identify and
correct technical problems affecting their performance. This is basically a technical assistance function by government.
Enforcement, on the other hand, is an oversight process of taking informative (``warning/information gathering
letters'') or punitive actions to ensure the public's desired objectives (``reliable data'') are achieved. QA/QC
and enforcement are different functions and need to be kept separate. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
4.7.2&blank;Defining Enforceable Violations 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The NELAP will need to specify what actions by laboratories will result in enforcement action. Furthermore, enforcement
actions should be developed in increasing severity to allow laboratory correction with minimal enforcement effort.
This could be done with tiers of enforcement actions, e.g. warning letter, suspension investigation order, suspension
order, and suspension hearing. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Enforceable violations will also need to be established to provide the basis for the enforcement program. Categories
of enforceable violations could include: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) Data falsification_intentional, by lab management, by employees, etc.; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) False advertising_misinforming clients regarding their accreditation and capabilities; and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) Continuing technical problems_lack of technical staff, failure to follow required SOP's, lack of equipment,
etc. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
4.7.3&blank;Recommendation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Given resource constraints, strong interest in encouraging state support, and the greater potential for implementation
in the mid-term (2 to 5 years), a variation of the decentralized option is recommended. This approach will still require
a federal-state laboratory integrated effort to ensure the objectives, structure, and issues are defined in the
necessary detail.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00194 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=84 g=1 f=1 -->
5.0&blank;Quality Systems 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
5.1&blank;Introduction 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Quality Systems include all quality assurance (QA) policies 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and quality control (QC) procedures, which shall be delineated 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in a QA Plan to help ensure and document the quality of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
analytical data. These shall include QA policies, which will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
establish essential QC procedures applicable to environmental 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratories regardless of size and complexity. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratory shall meet any additional or more stringent 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements as specified by the analytical methods, specific 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
programs or Agencies. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
All items identified in this discussion shall be available for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on-site inspection or data audit. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
5.2&blank;Quality System 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5.2.1&blank;Quality Assurance Plan 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
All laboratories shall prepare and have available for review 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a written description of the laboratory's quality assurance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
activities, i.e., a QA plan. The QA plan must be an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
independent document that may incorporate by reference, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
already available standard operating procedures (SOPs) or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
other material, e.g., methods, guidance documents, etc., that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are approved by the laboratory management. Analysts in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratory should either have copies of the document or easy 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
access to the document. The items listed below constitute 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
essential requirements of a Quality System. All laboratories 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
should be encouraged to add any additional items thought to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
improve the analytical data. The following items shall be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
included:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 04 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=25 g=1 f=1 -->
_General QC procedures 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Performance evaluation samples 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Staff 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Equipment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Test methods &amp; standard operating procedures (SOPs) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Physical facilities 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Sample acceptance policy &amp; sample receipt 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Sample tracking 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Record keeping, data review and reporting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Corrective action policy and procedures 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Definition of terms 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Bibliography 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
5.3&blank;General Quality Control Procedures 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The following are the essential requirements and routines to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
calculate and assess analytical precision, accuracy, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
method detection limits. All records and related quality 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
control procedures shall be documented and maintained. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The required essential quality control shall be as specified 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in the analytical methods or as listed below, whichever is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
more stringent. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5.3.1&blank;Chemical Testing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(a) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Method Reagent Blanks
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
_A minimum of 1 per batch of 20 or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
less samples per matrix type per sample extraction or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
preparation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Matrix Spikes (MS), Matrix Spike Duplicates (MSDs), and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Sample Duplicates (SD)
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
i. Matrix spikes: required frequency as per the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
method reagent blank, except for analytes for which 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards are not available (BOD, TSS, O&amp;G, and pH, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
etc.). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ii. Matrix spike duplicates or sample duplicates shall 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be analyzed at the same frequency as the original 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
matrix spike (MS). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Laboratory Fortified Blanks
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
_(QC Check Samples). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00195 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
It is suggested that these be analyzed at the same 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
frequency as the matrix spikes, but are mandatory if the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
matrix spikes are not within quality control acceptance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
limits. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(d) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Surrogates
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
_Surrogate compounds must be added to all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
samples, standards, and blanks whenever possible for all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
organic chromatography methods. Limits must be used to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
determine acceptable surrogate recoveries on a daily 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
basis. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(e) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Quality Control Validation Studies or Initial 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Demonstration of Analytical Capability
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
_QC Validation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Studies shall be performed on a one-time basis (initially 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and with a significant change, e.g., new analyst, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
instrument or technique). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(f) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Methods Used to Assess Precision and Accuracy
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
_The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratories shall calculate and track precision and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accuracy of test measurements and the associated 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
acceptance ranges using the data from the duplicate, MS, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
blank and surrogate measurements. The resulting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
acceptance ranges (and/or quality control charts) shall 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be used to assess data acceptance and shall be readily 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accessible in an identifiable file to all personnel 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
involved with the data review/data acceptance process. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(g) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Method Detection Limits
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
_Method detection limits shall 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be determined by an approved protocol or by a method 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
specified by the accrediting authority. The detection 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
limit is to be determined for the compounds of interest 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in each method in laboratory pure water and the matrix of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
interest. The procedure used must be documented. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(h) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Qualitative Identifications
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
_Qualitative quality control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
refers to the identification of a specific compound. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Identification of all analytes must be accomplished with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a verified standard of the analyte. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
When analyzing a new matrix, a new analyte or where other 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reasons for doubt exists, a confirmatory analysis shall 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be performed. Such analysis shall be a technique with a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
different scientific principle and may include:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 02 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=15 g=1 f=1 -->
_Second column confirmation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Alternate wavelengths 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Derivatization 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Mass spectral interpretation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Alternate detectors 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Additional cleanup procedures
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 02 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(i) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Reagent Quality, Water Quality and Checks
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
i. Reagents_In methods where the purity of reagents 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is not specified, analytical reagent grade shall be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
used. Reagents of lesser purity than that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
specified by the method shall not be used. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
labels on the container should be checked and the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
contents examined to verify that the purity of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reagents meets the needs of the particular method. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ii. Water_Where the method does not specify the type 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of water (e.g., distilled, deionized, etc.), the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
water quality shall be free from all constituents 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that may potentially interfere with the sample 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
preparation or analytical test. The quality of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
water sources shall be monitored and documented. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(j) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Glassware Cleaning
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
_In the analysis of samples 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
containing components in the parts per billion range, the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
preparation of scrupulously clean glassware is mandatory. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Particular care must be taken with glassware such as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Soxhlet extractors, Kuderna-Danish evaporative 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
concentrators, sampling-train components, or any other 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
glassware coming in contact with an extract that will be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
evaporated to a lesser volume. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00196 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Any cleaning and storage procedures that are not specified by the method shall be documented in laboratory records
and SOPs. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(k) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Internal Audits
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
_The laboratory shall have a system in place for conducting internal audits of the methods, data, and staff employed
at the lab. The audits shall be conducted at least twice annually and the results shall be documented. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5.3.2&blank;Bioassays 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(a) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Dilution Water Control
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
_Every toxicity test or range-finding test shall include a dilution water control treatment consisting of the same
dilution water, conditions, procedures, types and number of organisms as used in the effluent treatments, except
that none of the effluent being tested shall be added to the dilution water. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Whenever artificial sea salts are used in the salinity adjustment of either the dilution water sample or effluent
sample, an additional control treatment shall be included. This additional control treatment shall consist of replicate
chambers containing only artificial saltwater made with the same artificial sea salts used to adjust the samples.
The artificial saltwater shall be made to the same standardized salinity and Ph as the other test treatments. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Distribution of Test Organisms
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
_Test organisms must be randomly distributed to the test chambers either by: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
i. Adding to each chamber no more than 20% of the total number to be assigned to each chamber, then repeating the process
until each test chamber contains the total number of test organisms desired; or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ii. Randomly assigning one test organism to each test chamber, then randomly assigning a second test organism to each
test chamber, etc., continuing the random assignments until the total number of test organisms desired has been distributed
to each test chamber. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Dissolved Oxygen Requirement
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
_The DO in the test chambers shall be maintained at greater than 40% of saturation but less than 100% when testing chronic
toxicity for all species except 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Ceriodaphnia
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 which must be adjusted only prior to test initiation or sample renewal. Acute tests shall assure that a minimum level
of 4.0 mg/L DO is maintained. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(d) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Duplicate Requirements
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
_When the purpose of a definitive acute toxicity test is to determine compliance with an LC50, or EC50 permit limitation,
the test shall consist of one or more control treatments and a series of at least five effluent concentrations, in duplicate.

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
i. If the toxicity of the effluent to the test organism is not known, then the concentration of effluent in each treatment,
except for the highest concentration and the control(s) shall be at least 50% of the next higher one. The concentrations
selected shall be evenly spaced on either a logarithmic or geometric scale. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ii. Definitive test concentration series must, at a minimum, be conducted in duplicate. Additional replicate series
may be necessary in order to achieve required test precision. Only true replicates, with no water connections between
test chambers shall be used. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
iii. A minimum of twenty test organisms shall be exposed to each effluent concentration and each control treatment;
this means, when conducting the test in duplicate, at least ten organisms per test chamber. The number of organisms
used in each effluent concentration shall be equal to the number used in other effluent concentrations and to the number
used in the control. Organism loading limits shall be observed. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(e) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
No Measurable Acute Toxicity
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
_When the purpose of ``no measurable acute toxicity (N.M.A.T.) is to determine compliance with a N.M.A.T. permit
limitation, the effluent must be known to generally have an LC50 of greater than or equal to 100%, and the toxicity test
design must comply with the following: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
i. The test series shall consist of one or more control treatments, a 100% effluent-by-volume concentration and a
50% effluent-by-volume concentration. The test shall be conducted with at least four replicates, and at least ten
organisms per chamber. Additional duplicate series may be necessary in order to achieve required test precision.
Only true duplicates, with no water connections between test chambers, shall be used. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00197 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
ii. Forty or more test organisms shall be exposed to each control treatment and each effluent treatment. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(f) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Range Finding Toxicity Test
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
_If required by the accrediting agency and in the event historical aquatic toxicological data are not available on
an effluent, the lab shall conduct a range finding toxicity test to ascertain the range of effluent concentrations
for subsequent definitive tests. Range finding toxicity tests shall at a minimum consist of one or more control treatments,
and treatments of 100% effluent-by-volume, 50% effluent-by-volume, and 12.5% effluent-by-volume. A single test
series is adequate, although duplicates may be used. Five or more test organisms shall be exposed to each control treatment
and each effluent treatment. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(g) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Species Identification 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
i. For species identification, the laboratory shall maintain or have access to a type specimen collection. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ii. The laboratory must, at a specified frequency, use taxonomic experts to corroborate species identification.
In-house or outside experts are acceptable for taxonomic identification of test species. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(h) Criteria for Test Types
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
_All definitive acute toxicity tests and N.M.A.T definitive acute toxicity tests must be conducted as either static
non-renewal, static-renewal, or flow-through tests. Range-finding toxicity tests (if required) must be conducted
as either static or flow-through. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(i) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Reference Toxicants
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
_Reference toxicants shall be used as specified by method. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5.3.3&blank;Microbiology 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(a) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Blanks (Sterility Checks) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
i. Membrane Filter (MF) Analysis Blank_A membrane filter sterile control test of rinse water, media and supplies
shall be inoculated with at least 10 milliliters of sterile phosphate buffered dilution water (dilution blank control).
These shall be performed at the beginning and end of all processed samples and after every tenth sample. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ii. Multiple Tube Fermentation (MTF) Analysis Blank_A MTF blank shall be performed with each MTF sample. A single
tube of LTB broth media shall be inoculated with 10 milliliters of sterile phosphate buffered dilution water (dilution
blank control). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) Laboratory Pure (Reagent) Water Requirements 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
i. Laboratory pure water shall be analyzed annually by the Suitability Test for bactericidal properties for distilled
water. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ii. Laboratory pure water shall be analyzed monthly for pH, chlorine residual, standard plate count, and conductivity.

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
iii. The laboratory pure water must be analyzed annually for trace metals. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) MPN Analysis
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
_The MPN test for all water samples shall be completed on 10% of positive confirmed samples, except that gram staining
need not be performed for drinking water samples. If no positive tubes result from the tested drinking water samples,
the complete MPN test, but not gram staining, must be performed on a quarterly basis on at least one positive water source.

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00198 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(d) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
MF Analysis
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
_5% of all positive environmental samples analyzed and at least 10 of the sheen colonies for drinking water by membrane
filter shall be verified per method requirements. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(e) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Duplicates
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
_At least 5% of the positive samples shall be duplicated. In laboratories with more than one analyst, have each make
parallel analyses on at least one positive sample per month. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(f) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Positive and Negative Controls
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
_Positive and negative control cultures shall be analyzed for the microorganisms under test for each lot of media
used with each analytical procedure. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5.3.4&blank;Radiochemistry 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(a) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Instrument Blanks
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
_Instrument blanks are blanks at the background levels for any of the nuclide emission of interest. Instrument blanks
consist of a clean planchet, ampule or sealed canister that is placed in the instrument to duplicate sample counting
geometry. The purpose of the instrument blank is to verify instrument operation and ensure that no contamination
has occurred in the counting chamber. Instrument blanks are used for calculation of lower limits of detection. The
frequency of instrument analysis depends on the type of instrument. Essential frequencies for analysis of instrument
blanks on typical instruments are: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<TABLE>

<!-- PJG tableformat 2,L2,tp0,i1,s50,xls40 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=95 g=1 f=1 -->
&blank;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 007 0150 intable -->
1Instrument 
<!-- PJG 007 0150 intable -->
1Frequency 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 007 0152 intable -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=01 g=1 f=1 -->
Gamma spectrometers 
<!-- PJG 007 0104 intable -->
Monthly.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Low background proportional counters 
<!-- PJG 007 0104 intable -->
Daily.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Low level liquid scintillation counters 
<!-- PJG 007 0104 intable -->
Daily.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Scintillation counters 
<!-- PJG 007 0104 intable -->
Weekly.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Alpha spectrometers 
<!-- PJG 007 0104 intable -->
Weekly.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Radon flask counters 
<!-- PJG 007 0104 intable -->
Monthly.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</TABLE>

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(b) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Method Blanks_
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The required frequency for method blanks shall be at least once each batch or one out of every 20 samples, whichever
is greater. These specifications are applicable to all radiochemistry techniques except for gamma spectroscopy
where no chemical separation or other chemical manipulation is performed. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Laboratory Control Samples (LCS)_
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
At least one LCS shall be included with each batch or one out of every 20 analytical samples, whichever is greater. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(d) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Matrix Spikes_
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Matrix spikes shall be included with each sample batch where chemical manipulations and separations are performed.
The frequency for measurement of matrix spikes shall be at least one per batch or one out of every 20 samples, whichever
is greater. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The following criteria is recommended for spiking: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
i. Samples should be spiked at random within each batch. There should be adequate samples available for duplicate
analysis, if necessary. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ii. Spikes should be prepared in a manner to minimize alteration of the original matrix (i.e., minimize dilution of
the sample during the spiking). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
iii. Spikes should be prepared at a level that is at least two times the concentration of the analyte of interest. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(e) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Laboratory Duplicates_
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Sample analysis shall be duplicated on a randomly selected sample (not field blanks) within every batch or one per
20 samples, whichever is greater. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5.3.5&blank;Air Testing_To be added as document undergoes review. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00199 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
5.4&blank;Performance Evaluation Samples 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Each laboratory shall participate in a performance evaluation program as outlined in Chapter 2.0. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
5.5&blank;Environmental Laboratory Staffing Requirements
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5.5.1&blank;General Requirements for Laboratory Staff 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The testing laboratory shall have sufficient supervisory and other personnel, having the necessary education,
training, technical knowledge and experience for their assigned functions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Job descriptions shall be available for all positions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The laboratory shall have available a clear description of the lines of responsibility in the laboratory and shall
be proportioned such that adequate supervision is ensured. An organizational chart is recommended. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5.5.2&blank;Laboratory Staff Responsibilities and Credentials 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Laboratory management shall be responsible for: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) All analytical and operational activities of the laboratory, including those of any auxiliary or mobile laboratory
facilities; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) Supervision of all personnel employed by the laboratory, including those assigned to work in any auxiliary or
mobile laboratory facilities, and those persons designated as principle analysts; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) Assuring that all sample acceptance criteria (Section 5.9) are met and that samples are logged into the sample
tracking system and properly labeled and stored; and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(d) The production and quality of all data reported by the laboratory, including any auxiliary or mobile laboratory
facilities. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Each analyst and other members of the staff shall be responsible for complying with all QA requirements. Each laboratory
position must have a combination of experience and education to adequately demonstrate a specific knowledge of their
particular function and a general knowledge of laboratory operations, analytical methods, quality assurance/quality
control procedures and records management. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5.5.3&blank;Quality Assurance Officer 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
A quality assurance officer shall: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) Serve as the focal point for QA/QC and be responsible for analytical data review (sign off on data is required);

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) Have functions independent from laboratory management; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) Be able to objectively evaluate data and perform assessments without outside (e.g., managerial) influence;

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(d) Have formal training and experience in QA/QC procedures and be knowledgeable in the quality system as defined
under NELAP; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(e) Have a general knowledge of the analytical methods for which data review is performed; and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(f) Conduct internal audits on the entire operation twice annually. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
5.6&blank;Equipment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
A laboratory must have access to all equipment specified by the analytical procedures for which accreditation is
sought. All maintenance activities, both routine and nonroutine, shall be documented. The following records shall
be maintained for each piece of equipment:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 04 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=25 g=1 f=1 -->
_Name of item; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Manufacturer's name, type identification and serial number; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Date received and placed in service; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00200 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=25 g=1 f=1 -->
_Current physical location;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Maintenance log; and
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Calibration information, if appropriate.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
5.7&blank;Test Methods and Standard Operating Procedures
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
When the use of approved methods for a specific sample matrix is required, only those methods shall be used. In addition,
where performance-based methods or non-legally mandated methods are permitted, the relevant start-up and ongoing
validation procedures, and calibrations as specified in 5.7.2 must be followed and documented.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The criteria listed in 5.7 must be met for all methods and SOPs.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5.7.1&blank;Laboratory Method Manual(s) and Standard Operating Procedures
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Each certified laboratory shall have and maintain an in-house methods manual(s) and SOPs. The methods manual(s)
and any associated reference works (if required) shall be available to the bench analyst.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For each analyte certified, a method or methods to be used by the laboratory shall be described in the 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
methods manual.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 The method description shall include:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 04 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=25 g=1 f=1 -->
_Analyte name and qualifier (the qualifier is a word, phrase or number that better identifies the method; e.g., ``Iron,
Total'', or ``Chloride, Automated Ferricyanide'', or ``Our Lab. Method SOP No. 101'');
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Applicable matrix or matrices;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Method detection limit;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Scope and application;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Summary of the method;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Definitions;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Interferences;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Safety;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Equipment and supplies;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Reagents and standards;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Sample collection, preservation, shipment and storage;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Quality control;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Calibration and standardization;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Procedure;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Data analysis and calculations;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Method performance;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Pollution prevention;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Waste management;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_References; and
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Any tables, diagrams, flowcharts and validation data.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5.7.2&blank;Method Validation/Initial Demonstration of Method Performance (Performance-Based Methods and
Non-Approved Methods)
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Prior to acceptance and institution of any method, satisfactory initial demonstration of method performance, in
conformance with the relevant EPA guidelines, is required. In the absence of method-specified requirements, this
demonstration shall follow the outlined protocols of Paragraph 8.1.1 and Section 8.2 in the methods published in
40 CFR Part 136, Appendix A. Thereafter, continuing demonstration of method performance, in conformance with the
relevant EPA guidelines, is required. In both cases, the appropriate standard Performance Based Method System (PBMS)
checklist (see Appendix B) must be completed, submitted to the accrediting organization, and a copy must be retained
in the laboratory. All associated supporting data necessary to reproduce the analytical results summarized in the
checklists must be retained by the laboratory. Initial demonstration of method performance must be completed each
time there is a change in equipment, personnel or procedure.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00201 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5.7.3&blank;Calibration
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
5.7.3.1&blank;Documentation and Labeling
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The laboratory shall retain records (e.g., manufacturer's statement of purity), of the origin, purity and traceability
of all standards and reagents (including balance weights and thermometers). These records shall include the date
of receipt, the date of opening and an expiration date.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Detailed records shall be maintained on reagent and standard preparation. These records shall indicate traceability
to purchase stocks or neat compounds, and must include the date of preparation and preparer's initials.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Where calibrations do not include the generation of a standard curve (e.g., thermometers, balances, titrations,
etc.), records shall indicate the calibration date and type (e.g. balance weight, thermometer serial number, primary
standard concentration, etc.) of calibration standard that was used.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
All prepared reagents and standards shall be clearly identified with preparation date, concentration(s) and preparer's
initials.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
All standard curves shall be dated and labeled with method, analyte and standard concentrations and instrument responses.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The axes of the calibration curve should be labeled. For electronic data processing systems, that automatically
compute the calibration curve, the equation for the curve and the correlation coefficient must be recorded. The equation
for the line and the correlation coefficient shall also be recorded when the calibration curve is prepared manually.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
A criteria for an acceptable correlation coefficient shall be established.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5.7.3.2&blank;Initial Calibrations
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
All initial calibrations shall be verified with standards of high quality obtained from a second or different source.
These verification standards shall be analyzed with each initial calibration or quarterly, whichever is more frequent.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Standard curves shall be prepared as specified in the method.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The lowest standard should approach the method detection limit.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
If a method does not provide guidance in the preparation of a standard curve, the following guidelines shall be followed:
For all methods, use a blank and at least three (3) standards that lie within the linear portion of the curve. Additional
standards are required for non-linear calibration curves. In all cases, the sample results must be closely bracketed
by calibration standards.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
A new curve shall be run if two successive runs of one continuing calibration check is outside acceptable limits.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5.7.3.3&blank;Continuing Calibration Verification
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
When an initial calibration curve is not run on the day of analysis, the integrity of the initial calibration curve
shall be verified on each day of use (or 24 hour period) by initially analyzing a blank and a standard at a concentration
equal to or near the lowest calibration standard (the lowest calibration standard shall be in the range of 4 to 8 times
the calculated method detection limit).
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Additional standards shall be analyzed after the initial calibration curve or the integrity of the initial calibration
curve (see previous paragraph) has been accepted.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00202 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(a) These standards shall be analyzed at a frequency of 5% or every 8 hours whichever is more frequent and may be standards
used in the original calibration curve or standards from another source. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) The concentration of these standards shall be determined by the anticipated or known concentration of the samples.
To the extent possible, the samples in each interval (i.e. every 20 samples or every 8 hours) should be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
bracketed with standard concentrations closely representing the lower and upper range of reported sample concentrations.
If this is not possible, the standard calibration checks should vary in concentration throughout the range of the
data being acquired. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
When not specified by the analytical method, these calibration verification standards shall be within 15% of the
true value. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
5.8&blank;Physical Facilities
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5.8.1&blank;Environment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The laboratory facilities shall be maintained to permit the production of analytical data of needed quality. In addition
to adequate housekeeping that must be performed to assure that contamination is unlikely, the following elements
shall be controlled:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 04 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=25 g=1 f=1 -->
_Temperature; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Humidity; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Electrical power; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Vibration; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Electromagnetic fields; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Dust; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Direct sunlight; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Ventilation (exhaust hoods, air exchangers, etc.); and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Lighting. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5.8.2&blank;Work Area 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Adequate work spaces to ensure an unencumbered work area must be available. These include:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 04 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=25 g=1 f=1 -->
_Controlled access to the laboratory; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Separation of incompatible analyses; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Sample receipt area; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Sample storage area; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Chemical and waste storage area(s); and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Data handling and storage area(s). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00203 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
5.9&blank;Sample Acceptance Policy and Sample Receipt 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Regardless of the laboratory's level of control over sampling activities, the following are essential to ensure
sample integrity and valid data. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5.9.1&blank;Sample Acceptance Policy 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The laboratory shall have a written sample acceptance policy that clearly outlines the circumstances under which
samples will be accepted. Data from any samples which do not meet the following criteria must be flagged in an unambiguous
manner clearly defining the nature and substance of the variation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
This document should be circulated to sample collecting personnel with other sampling instructions and shall include
the following areas of concern: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) Submittal of field quality control samples as required by the accrediting agency. The samples may include trip
blanks, field blanks, equipment blanks, duplicates or other field-submitted quality control measures; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) Proper, full, and complete documentation, which shall include sample identification, the location, date and
time of collection, collector's name, preservative added, sample type and any special remarks concerning the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sample; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) Proper sample labeling to include unique identification and a labeling system for the samples with requirements
concerning the durability of the labels (water resistant) and the use of indelible ink; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(d) Evidence of proper preservation and use of appropriate sample containers. The type of sample containers and preservatives
are as specified by the individual programs, a Performance Based Method System or NELAP; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(e) Adherence to specified holding times. The maximum allowable holding time prior to analyses are as specified by
individual Programs, a Performance Based Method System or NELAP; and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(f) Adequate sample volume. Sufficient sample volume must be available to perform the necessary analysis. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5.9.2&blank;Sample Receipt Protocols 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Samples shall be checked upon receipt for thermal preservation (if applicable) and all other aforementioned items.
Chemical preservation (e.g., appropriate Ph) shall be checked upon receipt or prior to sample preparation/analyses.
The results of such checks shall be recorded. Data from any samples which do not meet the criteria must be flagged in
an unambiguous 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manner clearly defining the nature and substance of the variation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
If applicable, a complete chain of custody record (Section 5.11.3) shall be maintained. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5.9.3&blank;Storage Conditions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The samples shall be properly preserved and stored in approved containers specified by the individual EPA or state
programs, the Performance Based Method System or NELAP. Samples shall be stored in a secure area. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
5.10&blank;Sample Tracking 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The laboratory shall design a system to unequivocally identify all samples, subsamples and subsequent extracts
and/or digestates so that each aliquot is uniquely identified. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The laboratory shall assign a unique identification (ID) code to each sample container received in the laboratory.
Multiple aliquots of a sample that have been received for different analytical tests (e.g., nutrients, metals, VOCs,
etc.) must be assigned a different ID code. The use of container shape, size or other physical characteristic (e.g.,
amber glass, purple top, etc.) is not an acceptable means of identifying the sample. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
This laboratory code shall maintain an unequivocal link with the unique field ID assigned each container. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The laboratory ID number shall be placed on the sample container as a durable label. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The laboratory ID number shall be entered into the laboratory records (see 5.11.2) and shall be the link that associates
the sample with related laboratory activities (i.e., sample preparation, calibration, etc.). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In cases where the sample collector and analyst are the same individual or the laboratory preassigns numbers to sample
containers, the laboratory ID number may be the same as the field ID number. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5.11&blank;Record Keeping, Data Review and Reporting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The laboratory shall implement protocols that will produce unequivocal, accurate records which document all laboratory
activities associated with sample receipt, preparation, analysis, review and reporting. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
There are two levels of record keeping: (1) Sample custody or tracking and (2) legal or evidentiary chain of custody.
All essential requirements for sample custody are outlined in Sections 5.11.1.1, and 5.11.1.2. The basic requirements
for legal chain of custody (if required or implemented) are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
specified in Section 5.11.3. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5.11.1&blank;Sample Custody Requirements 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
5.11.1.1&blank;Essential Documentation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(a) Sample Handling_Sample custody shall document all procedures and activities to which a sample is subjected.
These activities shall include but are not limited to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=25 g=1 f=1 -->
_Sample preservation including appropriate sample container and compliance with holding time; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Sample identification, receipt, acceptance or rejection and log-in; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Sample storage and tracking (includes shipping receipts, transmittal forms, and internal routing and assignment
records); 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00204 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=25 g=1 f=1 -->
_Sample preparation (includes cleanup and separation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
protocols, ID 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=25 g=6 f=1 -->
#
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=25 g=1 f=1 -->
s, volumes, weights, instrument 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
printouts, meter readings, calculations, reagents, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
etc.); 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Sample analysis; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Standard and reagent origin, receipt, preparation, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and use; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Equipment receipt, use, specification, operating 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
conditions and preventative maintenance; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Calibration criteria, frequency and acceptance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
criteria; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Data and statistical calculations, review, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
confirmation, interpretation, assessment and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reporting conventions; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Method performance criteria including expected 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
quality control requirements; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Quality control protocols and assessment; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Electronic data security, software documentation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and verification, software and hardware audits, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
backups, and records of any changes to automated 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
data entries; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_All automated sample handling systems; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Records storage and retention; and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Sample disposal including the date of sample or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
subsample disposal and name of the responsible 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
person.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 04 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(b) Laboratory Support Activities_In addition to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
documenting all the above-mentioned activities, the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
following shall be retained: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=25 g=1 f=1 -->
_All original raw data, whether hard copy or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
electronic, for calibrations, samples and quality 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
control measures, including analysts work sheets 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and data output records (chromatograms, strip 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
charts, and other instrument response readout 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
records); 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Copies of final reports; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Archived standard operating procedures; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Correspondence relating to laboratory activities 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for a specific project; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_All corrective action reports, audits and audit 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
responses; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Performance evaluation results and raw data; and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Data review and cross checking.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 04 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(c) Analytical Records_The essential information to be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
recorded on all raw data associated with analysis (e.g., 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
strip charts, tabular printouts, computer data files, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
analytical notebooks, run logs, etc.) shall include:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 04 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=25 g=1 f=1 -->
_Laboratory sample ID number; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Date of analysis; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Instrumentation identification and instrument 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operating conditions/parameters (or reference to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
such data); 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Analysis type; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_All calculations (automated and manual); and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Analyst's or operator's initials/signature. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5.11.1.2&blank;Record Keeping System and Design 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Each organization shall design and maintain a record keeping 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
system that is succinct, self-explanatory and efficient and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
allows historical reconstruction of all laboratory activities 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that produced the resultant sample analytical data. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
history of the sample must be readily understood through the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
documentation. This shall include interlaboratory transfers 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of samples and/or extracts. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
All information relating to the laboratory facilities 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
equipment, analytical methods, and related laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
activities (e.g., sample receipt, sample preparation, data 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
review, etc.) shall be documented. All documentation shall be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
maintained to reflect current operating protocols. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The organization should establish essential personnel 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
qualifications and shall maintain records on personnel 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
training. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00205 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Organizations shall maintain standard operating procedures 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(SOPs) that accurately reflect all phases of current 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratory activities including assessing data integrity. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) These documents may be specific sample preparation or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
analytical references, (e.g., analytical method numbers), 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
equipment manuals (provided by the manufacturer), or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
internally written documents. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) The SOPs shall also include a list of analytical methods 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that are used by the laboratory. This list shall be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
indexed according to NELAC accreditation categories 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(e.g., drinking water, solid waste, etc.). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) In cases where minor modifications to accepted methods 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
have been made (e.g., change in type of column, change in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operating conditions, etc.), or where the referenced 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
method is ambiguous or provides insufficient detail 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(e.g., reagent purity, reagent concentration, etc.), 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
these changes or clarifications shall be documented as an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
appendix to the referenced method. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Copies of the above-mentioned SOPs shall be accessible to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
workplace. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The record keeping system shall facilitate the retrieval of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
all working files and archived records for inspection and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
verification purposes. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
All documentation entries shall be signed or initialed by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
responsible staff. The reason for the signature or initials 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
shall be clearly indicated in the records (e.g., sampled by, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prepared by, reviewed by, etc.). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Entries into all records shall be legibly written in indelible 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ink. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Entries in records shall not be obliterated by erasures or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
markings. All corrections to record-keeping errors shall be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
made by one line marked through the error. The individual 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
making the correction shall sign (or initial) and date the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
correction. These criteria also shall apply to electronically 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
maintained records. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5.11.1.3&blank;Laboratory Report Format and Contents 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The laboratory shall report results, accurately, clearly, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
unambiguously and objectively and in a manner that is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
understandable to the recipient. The basic information to be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
included in the report includes the following: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) Report title (e.g., ``Certificate of Results'', ``Laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Results'', etc.) with the name, address and phone number 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the laboratory (or laboratories, see subcontracted 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratories below); 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) Name and address of client and/or project; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) Description and identification of sample (including 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
client ID number); 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(d) Date of sample receipt, sample collection and sample 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
analysis; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(e) Time of sample preparation and/or analysis if the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
required holding time for either activity is less than or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
equal to 48 hours; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(f) Test method or unambiguous description of any non-standard method; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(g) Test results with any failures or deviations from methods 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or quality control criteria identified (i.e., data 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
qualifiers); 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00206 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(h) Signature and title of individual(s) accepting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
responsibility for the content of the report and date of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
issue; and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(i) Clear identification of any results that were performed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
by a subcontracted laboratory. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
If appropriate, the laboratory shall certify that the test 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
results meet all requirements of NELAP or provide reasons 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and/or justification if they do not. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Once issued, the laboratory report shall remain unchanged. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Any corrections, additions and/or deletions from the original 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reports shall be supported by supplementary documentation, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
shall clearly identify its purpose, and shall contain all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reporting requirements specified above. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5.11.1.4&blank;Records Management and Storage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(a) All records of an organization that are pertinent to a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
specified project shall be retained for a minimum of five 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
years unless otherwise designated for a longer period of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
time in another regulation. The records specified in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
5.11.1.1 and 5.11.1.2 above shall be retained. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) Records that are stored or generated by computers or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
personal computers (PCs) shall have hard copy and write-protected backup copies. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) When a procedure or document (e.g., initial calibration 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
records, SOPs, etc.) becomes obsolete or is replaced, the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
records shall clearly indicate the time period (or sample 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sets, if applicable) during which the procedure or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
document was in force. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(d) All access to archived information shall be documented. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(e) If an organization goes out of business or changes 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ownership before the time period for records retention 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
has expired, all documentation shall be transferred in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
whole to the archives of the sponsor (client) of the work 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or to the new owner as described in Section 4.1.8. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5.11.2&blank;Sample Custody Tracking and Data Documentation for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Laboratory Operations 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
5.11.2.1&blank;Sample Receipt, Log In and Storage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
All records pertinent to sample receipt, log in and storage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
shall be maintained. In addition, the laboratory shall: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) Retain all correspondence and/or official conversations 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
concerning the final disposition of rejected samples; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) Fully document any decision to proceed with the analysis 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of compromised samples:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 04 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=25 g=1 f=1 -->
_The condition of these samples shall be noted in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
all documentation associated with the sample. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_The analysis data shall be appropriately ``qualified 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
as estimated'' on all internal documentation and on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the final report.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 04 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(c) Utilize a permanent, chronological log to document 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
receipt of all sample containers. The following 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
information must be recorded in the laboratory sequential 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
log:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 04 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=25 g=1 f=1 -->
_Date of laboratory receipt of sample; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Sample collection date; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Unique laboratory ID code (see 5.10 above); 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Field ID code supplied by sample submitter; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Requested analyses, including approved method 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
number, if applicable; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Signature or initials of data logger; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Comments resulting from inspection for sample 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
acceptance rejection; and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_Sampling kit code (if applicable).
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 04 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00207 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(d) All documentation that is transmitted to the laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
by the sample transmitter shall be retained (e.g., memos, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
transmittal forms, etc.). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5.11.2.2&blank;Intralaboratory Distribution of Samples for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Analysis 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(a) The laboratory shall utilize a proactive procedure to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ensure that all samples and subsamples are analyzed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
within allowed maximum allowable holding times. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) All distribution of samples and subsamples for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
preparation and analysis shall be documented as to task 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assignment and analysis date deadline. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5.11.3&blank;Legal or Evidentiary Custody Procedures 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The use of legal chain of custody (COC) protocols is strongly 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
recommended and may be required by some state or federal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
programs. In addition to the records listed in 5.11.1.1 and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
5.11.1.2, the following protocols shall be incorporated if 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
legal COC is implemented by the organization. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5.11.3.1&blank;Basic Requirements 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The chain of custody records shall establish an intact, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
contiguous record of the physical possession, storage and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
disposal of sample containers, collected samples, sample 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
aliquots, and sample extracts or digestates. For ease of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
discussion, the above-mentioned items shall be referred to as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
samples: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) The COC records shall account for all time periods 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
associated with the samples. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) The COC records shall include signatures of all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
individuals who were involved with physically handling 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the samples. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) In order to simplify record-keeping, the number of people 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
who physically handle the sample should be minimized. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(d) The COC records are not limited to a single form or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
document. However, organizations should attempt to limit 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the number of documents that would be required to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
establish COC. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(e) Legal chain of custody shall begin at the point 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
established by the federal or state oversight program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
This may begin at the point that cleaned sample 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
containers are provided by the laboratory or the time 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sample collection occurs. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(f) The COC forms shall remain with the samples during 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
transport or shipment. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5.11.3.2&blank;Required Information in Custody Records 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
In addition to the information specified in 5.11.1.1 and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
5.11.1.2, tracking records shall include, by direct entry or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
linkage to other records: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) Time of day and calendar date of each transfer or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
handling procedure; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) Signatures of all personnel who physically handle the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sample(s); 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) All information necessary to produce unequivocal, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accurate records that document the laboratory activities 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
associated with sample receipt, preparation, analysis and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reporting; and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(d) Common carrier documents. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5.11.3.3&blank;Controlled Access to Samples 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Access to all legal samples and subsamples shall be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
controlled and documented. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00208 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5.11.3.4&blank;Transfer of Samples to Another Party 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Transfer of samples, subsamples, digestates or extracts to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
another party are subject to all of the requirements for legal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
chain of custody. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5.11.3.5&blank;Sample Disposal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(a) If the sample is part of litigation, disposal of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
physical sample shall occur only with the concurrence of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the affected legal authority, sample data user and/or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
submitter of the sample. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) All conditions of disposal and all correspondence between 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
all parties concerning the final disposition of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
physical sample shall be recorded and retained. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) Records shall indicate the date of disposal, the nature 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of disposal (i.e. sample depleted, sample disposed in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hazardous waste facility, sample returned to client, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
etc.), and the name of the individual who performed the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
task. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
5.12&blank;Corrective Action Policy and Procedures 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The laboratory shall develop contingencies for unacceptable 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
quality control results. These policies shall be specified in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
written SOPs and shall include the following: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) Identification of such problems, and the anticipated 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and/or recommended corrective actions to correct and/or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
eliminate future occurrences; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) Requirement for written records that document the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
problem, the corrective measures, and the final outcome; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) An established policy requiring that a laboratory does 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
not accept samples on a routine basis without the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
capability of meeting the maximum holding times. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00209 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=84 g=1 f=1 -->
Appendix A 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=73 g=1 f=1 -->
Definitions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=3 -->
Accreditation: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
The process by which an agency or organization 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
evaluates and recognizes a program of study or an institution 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
as meeting certain predetermined qualifications or standards, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
thereby accrediting the laboratory. In the context of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
National Environmental Laboratory Accreditation Program 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(NELAP), this process is a voluntary one. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=3 -->
Accreditation Authority Review Board: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
A five member group 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
appointed by EPA from the states, EPA, and other federal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
agencies to review the process and procedures used by EPA to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
approve state and federal laboratories and accreditation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
authorities. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=3 -->
Accrediting Authority:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
 The agency having responsibility and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accountability for environmental laboratory accreditation and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
who grants accreditation. For the purposes of NELAC, this is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA, other federal agencies, or the state. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=3 -->
Accrediting Body: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
The organization that actually executes the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accreditation process, i.e., receives and reviews 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accreditation applications, reviews QA documents, reviews 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
performance evaluation testing results, surveys the site, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
etc., whether EPA, the state, or contracted private party. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=3 -->
Accuracy: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
The degree of agreement between an observed value 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and an accepted reference value. Accuracy includes a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
combination of random error (precision) and systematic error 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(bias) components which are due to sampling and analytical 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operations; a data quality indicator. (Glossary of Quality 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Assurance Terms, QAMS, 8/31/92). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=3 -->
Administrative Committee: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
A committee of the National 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Environmental Laboratory Accreditation Conference involved 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
with the internal business affairs of the conference. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Currently, these are the Conference Management and Funding, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Nominating, Membership, Auditing, Liaison, and Contributor 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Committee. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=3 -->
Applicant: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
Any environmental laboratory seeking 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accreditation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=3 -->
Assessment: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
The physical process of inspecting, testing and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
documenting results from a laboratory for purposes of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accreditation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=3 -->
Assessment Team: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
An individual or group of individuals who 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
perform the on-site assessment of a laboratory. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=3 -->
Board of Directors: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
The guiding body of NELAC composed of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Director, Executive Secretary, Chair, Chair-elect, Past Chair, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Treasurer, and six at-large members. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=3 -->
Calibration Standard: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
A solution prepared from the primary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
dilution standard solution or stock standard solutions and the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
internal standards and surrogate analytes. The Calibration 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
solutions are used to calibrate the instrument response with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
respect to analyte concentration. (Glossary of Quality 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Assurance Terms, QAMS, 8/31/92). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=3 -->
CNAEL: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
The Committee on National Accreditation of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Environmental Laboratories chartered by EPA in 1991 to assess 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the need, feasibility, and practicability of a national 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
environmental laboratory accreditation program. Dissolved 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
after its report to EPA in September 1992. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=3 -->
Compromised Samples: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
Those samples which were improperly 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sampled, or with insufficient documentation (chain of custody 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and other sample records and/or labels), improper preservation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and/or containers were used, or the holding time has been 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exceeded. Under normal conditions compromised samples are not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
analyzed. If emergency situations require analysis, the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
results must be appropriately qualified. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00210 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=3 -->
Contracted Organization: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
A private accrediting body meeting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the standards for accreditation of environmental laboratories 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and employed by an accrediting authority to perform certain 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accrediting functions, e.g. on-site audits. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=3 -->
Contributors: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
Any person or group having an interest in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
environmental laboratory accreditation other than a state or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
federal official involved in environmental laboratory affairs, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
who may participate in the deliberations of the conference by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
presenting papers, debating issues, etc. but without vote or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
formal membership on a committee. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=3 -->
Deficiency Report: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
A report generated by the Inspector who is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a state employee or authorized agent of the state in response 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to deficiencies noted in the course of a laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessment, inspection or performance evaluation sample 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
analysis result. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=3 -->
Denial: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
The refusal to grant approval to all or part of a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratory's initial or subsequent application for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
certification by the National Environmental Laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Accreditation Program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=3 -->
Environmental Laboratory Advisory Board: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
The name of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Federal Advisory Committee Act body chartered by EPA and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
composed of special interest groups or persons to interact 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
with the Board of Directors. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=3 -->
Equipment Blank (Sample Equipment Blank): 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
A clean sample 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(e.g., distilled water) that is collected in a sample 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
container with the sample-collection device and returned to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the laboratory as a sample. Sampling equipment blanks are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
used to check the cleanliness of sampling devices. (Glossary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of Quality Assurance Terms, QAMS, 8/31/92). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=3 -->
Failure: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
Failing one or more of the criteria outlined in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
factors examined in announced and unannounced laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessments which include: competence of staff, qualifications 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of staff and supervisors, working conditions, equipment, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
supplies, supervision, methods used, quality assurance/quality 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
control procedures, recordkeeping, and compliance with good 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratory practices. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=3 -->
Field Blank: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
A clean sample (e.g., distilled water), carried 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to the sampling site, exposed to sampling conditions (e.g., 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
bottle caps removed, preservatives added) and returned to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratory and treated as an environmental sample. Field 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
blanks are used to check for analytical artifacts and/or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
background introduces by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sampling and analytical procedures. (Glossary of Quality 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Assurance Terms, QAMS, 8/31/92). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR941202-2-00211 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR941202-2-00098 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4703 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=3 -->
Holding Times (Maximum Allowable Holding Times): 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
The maximum 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
times that samples may be held prior to analysis and still be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
considered valid. (40 CFR Part 136). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=3 -->
Initial Demonstration of Analytical Capability: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
Procedure to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
establish the ability to generate acceptable accuracy and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
precision which is included in many of the EPA's analytical 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
methods. In general the procedure includes the addition of a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
specified concentration of each analyte (using a QC check 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sample) in each of four separate aliquots of laboratory pure 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
water. These are carried through the entire analytical 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
procedure and the percentage recovery and the standard 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
deviation are determined and compared to specified limits. (40 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
CFR Part 136). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=3 -->
Inspection Report: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
The written results listing specific 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
deficiencies and levels of performance that result from a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratory assessment. This is a public record document 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prepared by the inspector. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=3 -->
Inspector: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
The authorized representative of the appropriate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
department within a state who directly conducts the laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessment of inspection. This representative may be a third 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
party contractor to the state who inspects and acts under the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
authority of the state. All actions and requests made by such 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a third party are made under the regulatory authority of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
state. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=3 -->
Instrument Blank: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
A clean sample (e.g., distilled water) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
processed through the instrumental steps of the measurement 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
process; used to determine instrument contamination. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(Glossary of Quality Assurance Terms, QAMS, 8/31/92). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=3 -->
Laboratory:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
 A facility engaged in the collection or analysis 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and reporting of environmental samples, whether fixed or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
mobile. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=3 -->
Laboratory Control Sample (quality control sample): 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
An 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
uncontaminated sample matrix spiked with known amounts of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
analytes from a source independent of the calibration 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards. It is generally used to establish intra-laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or analyst specific precision and bias or to assess the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
performance of all or a portion of the measurement system. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(Glossary of Quality Assurance Terms, QAMS, 8/31/92). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=3 -->
Legal Chain of Custody (COC): 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
An unbroken trail of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accountability that ensures the physical security of samples, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
data and records. (Glossary of Quality Assurance Terms, QAMS, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
8/31/92). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=3 -->
Local: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
An individual state. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=3 -->
Manager: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
The individual designated as being responsible for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the overall operation, all personnel, and the physical plant 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the environmental laboratory. A supervisor may report to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the manager. In some cases, the supervisor and the manager 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
may be the same individual. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=3 -->
Matrix Spike (spiked sample, fortified sample): 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
Prepared by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adding a known mass of target analyte to a specified amount of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
matrix sample for which an independent estimate of target 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
analyte concentration is available. Matrix spikes are used, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for example, to determine the effect of the matrix on a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
method's recovery efficiency. (Glossary of Quality Assurance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Terms, QAMS, 8/31/92). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=3 -->
Matrix Spike Duplicate (spiked sample/fortified sample 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
duplicate): 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
A second replicate matrix spike is prepared and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
analyzed to obtain a measure of the precision of the recovery 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for each analyte. (Glossary of Quality Assurance Terms, QAMS, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
8/31/92). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=3 -->
Member (or active member): 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
A state or federal official 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
engaged in setting regulatory standards or accreditation of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
environmental laboratories, eligible for committee assignment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and having voting privileges in the NELAC. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



